'MegaNet -- base-station-http.CR300
'
'   A radio base station's half of MegaNet's ingest: read what the ALERT2
'   receiver hears off RS-232, read the sensors wired to this logger's own
'   terminals, and send both to MegaNet over BOTH inbound paths -- POST to
'   meganet.ingest_http(), and publish to the MQTT bridge.
'
' Target: CR300-series. "Other dataloggers" at the foot says what to change for
' a CR1000X or a CR6. docs/ingest-http.md and docs/ingest-mqtt.md are the two
' contracts this program has to satisfy; logger/README.md is the commissioning
' guide, including the Device Configuration Utility settings that have to be
' right before either half of this can deliver anything at all.
'
' --- Version 3.0: what came OUT, and why ------------------------------------
'
' **v2.1 would not fit on a CR300.** It compiled and ran out of memory, and the
' reason is worth writing down because it was not the new features -- they cost
' about 5 KB. It was that the program had never stopped being a bring-up tool.
'
' v1 was written not knowing what the receiver would send. Nobody knew whether
' the feed was ASCII or binary, what delimited it, how long a line was, or which
' of eight byte classes it used -- so the program was built to answer those
' questions: a byte census, a delimiter poll, a separator tally, a per-rejection
' counter, a hex dump of every block, a hex dump of the buffer, a hex dump of
' the last line, a hex dump of the last BAD line, the first line ever seen kept
' forever, a raw capture file on flash, and four tables recording all of it --
' RawLog, FrameLog, ReadingLog and LineLog, about 700 KB of table allocation
' between them.
'
' **Those questions are answered.** The receiver speaks binary; the frame layout
' is known and is documented below; the station has posted 7,887 readings from
' 685 addresses. A tool for answering a question that has been answered is not
' a diagnostic, it is 22 KB of a 30 KB budget. So this version deletes it, and
' what is deleted is recorded here rather than quietly dropped:
'
'   the raw capture file      CapStart/CapFlush/CaptureBlock and 3.6 KB of
'                             buffers. `git show ea4116d` has it if a future
'                             receiver ever needs the question asked again.
'   the byte census           charTally(126), RxByteClass(8), the separator
'                             tally, PickDelim. The feed is comma-delimited when
'                             it is ASCII at all; ParseLine now says so.
'   the why-counters          RxWhyCount(14)/RxWhyName(14). whyCode still
'                             records WHICH rejection; the tally of how many is
'                             what a capture was for.
'   nine hex/text displays    RxBlockHex, RxBlockHexFull, RxBlockText, RxBufHex,
'                             RxBufText, RxLastLineHex, RxLastBadHex,
'                             RxFirstLineHex, FrHex, FrElemHex -- 4.4 KB of
'                             strings, replaced by ONE: RxHex, the first 40
'                             bytes of the last frame.
'   four tables               RawLog, FrameLog, ReadingLog, LineLog.
'
' What did NOT come out: both receive paths, both transports, the two-tail ring
' buffer, the clock discipline, the local sensors, the self-test, and every
' Public that says what the program is doing right now. Nothing that reports on
' the LIVE system was cut -- only the machinery for working out what the system
' was.
'
' The other 9 KB came out of the ring buffer without losing a slot. See section 3.
'
' --- What this is -----------------------------------------------------------
'
' MegaNet has two inbound paths and they converge on one contract. HTTP needs no
' process anywhere: the datalogger posts straight at the database. MQTT needs a
' broker and bridge/ -- a subscriber that holds the subscription Postgres cannot
' hold -- and pays for them with what it gives back: the broker's Last Will says
' when this base station stopped talking, which no amount of HTTP tells anybody.
'
' This program does both, for the same readings, from one queue -- and there are
' two ways a reading gets into that queue:
'
'   field stations --radio--> ERT-A2 receiver --RS-232--> THIS PROGRAM
'                                                            ^
'    rain gauge, 2 x SDI-12 level, supply volts --cable------+
'                                                            |
'                                          +-----------------+-----------------+
'                                          |                                   |
'                                        HTTPS                         MQTT (QoS 1)
'                                          |                                   |
'                                          v                                   v
'                               meganet.ingest_http()                       broker
'                                          |                                   |
'                                          |                                bridge/
'                                          |                                   |
'                                          +-----------------+-----------------+
'                                                            |
'                                                    meganet.ingest()
'
' **Sending the same reading twice is not a mistake, it is the design.**
' meganet.reading's primary key is (address, instant, value), so the second copy
' of a reading is stored zero times and counted as a duplicate -- both contracts
' say so in as many words, which is what makes a belt-and-braces base station
' possible without an acknowledgement protocol between the two paths. Whichever
' copy lands first sets the row's `source` ("http" or "mqtt"); the other is
' counted and discarded. A path being down costs nothing but its own duplicates.
'
' --- The sensors on this logger's own terminals -----------------------------
'
' A base station is normally only a relay. This one also measures: a tipping
' bucket on the pulse terminal and two SDI-12 level sensors on C1, plus its own
' supply voltage, which needs nothing wired to it at all.
'
' Those readings have no ALERT address -- there is no packet and no transmitting
' node -- so they use the ingest contract's OTHER address shape, a station
' number plus a channel:
'
'   radio    {"alert_id":6270, ...}                              ->  a:6270
'   local    {"station_number":"999998","channel":"rain", ...}   ->  s:999998/rain
'
' Same queue, same batch, same POST, same idempotency. Section 1's local sensors
' block is the whole of the configuration, and it is also where the one place
' this program DOES convert is argued for: it knows the bucket size because the
' gauge is two metres away, which is exactly what a base station hearing forty
' sites does not know about any of them.
'
' --- Proving the whole path without waiting for a transmission --------------
'
' A base station with no radio traffic cannot be shown to work, and a workshop
' rig has none. So this program can transmit to itself: set TestFire true and it
' builds a complete, valid BINARY ALERT2 frame -- the shape this receiver
' actually emits -- and appends it to the receive byte buffer one instruction
' before the framer runs. TakeFrames, DecodeFrame, the bit unpacking, the clock
' gate, the queue, the batch and the POST all run on it exactly as they would on
' a real frame, because as far as every one of them can tell it IS one.
' docs/live-end-to-end-test.md is the card.
'
' **It builds the binary form and not the ASCII one deliberately**: the receiver
' on this base station speaks binary, so DecodeFrame is the live decoder and
' ParseAlert2 is not. A self-test that exercised the decoder nobody uses would
' be testing the wrong thing. The ASCII path is still there and still parsed --
' see the plain-line test in logger/README.md -- for a receiver configured that
' way.
'
' --- The four things this program is careful about --------------------------
'
' 1. It never blocks the scan on the network. Serial is read in the 1-second
'    main scan; the POST happens in a SlowSequence, because HTTPPost sits there
'    until the server answers and a base station that stops reading its serial
'    port for a minute has lost every transmission in that window. The SDI-12
'    bus is in a sequence of its own for the same reason.
'
' 2. It never throws a reading away because a link was down. Parsed readings go
'    into a ring buffer and stay there until they have been delivered. A failed
'    attempt is retried with backoff and the same batch is sent again -- both
'    contracts guarantee that re-sending the same reading stores it once, so
'    there is no acknowledgement protocol to build here.
'
'    The buffer has TWO readers, one per path, each with its own position in it,
'    its own backoff and its own idea of what it has delivered. A slot is only
'    reused once BOTH have passed it. That is what stops a broker outage from
'    stalling the POSTs, an HTTP outage from stalling the publishes, and -- the
'    failure a single shared position would have caused -- a stuck path from
'    pinning the other one to the same readings while newer ones pile up behind
'    it and fall off the end unsent.
'
' 3. It does not trust a clock it cannot check. The endpoint rejects anything
'    stamped before 1990, and the usual cause is an RTC that lost power. This
'    program disciplines its clock against NTP, and refuses to stamp a reading
'    until the clock passes a sanity check, rather than posting rubbish.
'
' 4. It does not convert what it did not measure. value_raw is the 11-bit number
'    that came off the air. A base station hearing forty sites does not know
'    which of them is a rain gauge and which is a level sensor, and a wrong
'    bucket size is worse than no bucket size -- MegaNet does the conversion
'    where it knows the sensor.
'
'    The sensors on its own terminals are the exception that proves the rule,
'    and they are the exception because the reasoning does not apply to them:
'    this logger is wired to them and the bucket size is written on the gauge.
'    So a local reading carries `value`, `unit` and `conversion` BESIDE its
'    value_raw and never instead of it -- the raw count is still what was
'    measured, and the rule that has to hold is that nothing is ever thrown
'    away, not that nothing is ever computed.
'
' The token is not in this file and never appears in a Public variable. It is
' read at startup out of a file on the logger, so this program can live in a
' public repository, be emailed, and be loaded onto a second base station
' without carrying a live credential.
'
' --- A note on what is hand-rolled and what is not --------------------------
'
' The substring search and the line splitter are written out of Mid(), Len() and
' ASCII() rather than calling InStr() and SplitStr(). Both of those take an
' option code, and a wrong option code does not fail to compile -- it silently
' parses the wrong thing, which for a program whose entire job is parsing is the
' worst failure mode available. Bit fields are pulled out with MOD and INT
' rather than with shift operators, for the same reason: CRBasic has no << or >>
' and the arithmetic form is unambiguous on every operating system.
'
' This file is deliberately plain ASCII throughout, so it reads the same in
' CRBasic Editor as it does on GitHub.
'
'==============================================================================


'==============================================================================
' 1. SITE CONFIGURATION -- the part you edit
'==============================================================================

' Which ingest point this is is at the top of the Public table -- see
' BASE_NAME there. It is Public rather than Const so the name can be corrected
' at the logger without recompiling.

' The endpoint, from docs/ingest-http.md. https, so TLS turns itself on.
Const INGEST_URI = "https://jjprlritvhdqpvphfrnu.supabase.co/rest/v1/rpc/ingest_http"

' Identifies the Supabase project. Not a secret -- it is committed to this
' repository and can neither read nor write anything on its own.
Const API_KEY = "sb_publishable_PV9VjCM8NQeGAJMuwa5TKA_yX9GWacY"

' Where the secret lives. One line, the token, nothing else. Load it with Device
' Configuration Utility (File Control); see logger/README.md. If the file is
' missing the program still runs and still fills its Readings table -- it just
' says "no token" in PostState and keeps queueing. CPU: is used because a CR300
' has no microSD slot, so CRD: does not exist on this hardware.
Const TOKEN_FILE = "CPU:meganet_token.txt"

' The wire protocol behind the RADIO readings, recorded against every one of
' them. "alert2" for an ERT-A2. Change it to "alert" if your decoder is feeding
' this program legacy ALERT, or "unknown" if you would rather not claim.
Const PROTOCOL = "alert2"

' Reported in the MQTT status message, so "which loggers are still on the old
' program" is answerable from the database rather than from a site visit. Bump
' it when you change this file in a way a field technician would care about.
Const FW_VERSION = "3.0"

' --- the serial port ---------------------------------------------------------
'
' 9600-8-N-1 is the ERT-A2's ALERT2 ASCII default. Format 3 is 8-N-1
' transparent, and it is the right one for a third-party feed: format 0 discards
' ASCII 0 and every byte above 127, and leaves the PakBus stack scanning the
' stream for frames it will never find.
Const RX_PORT = ComRS232
Const RX_BAUD = 9600
Const RX_FORMAT = 3
Const RX_TXDELAY = 0
Const RX_BUFFER = 2000             'port buffer, bytes. Two seconds of 9600 baud
                                   'is 1920 bytes, so this holds a full scan's
                                   'worth twice over

Const RX_BLOCK = 250               'most bytes taken from the port in one read
Const RX_MAX = 250                 'longest text line this program will assemble

' --- posting over HTTP -------------------------------------------------------
'
' MAX_BATCH came down from 25 at v3.0, with CONTENT_SIZE. A radio reading is 69
' characters of JSON and a local one about 180, so 12 of the longest possible
' plus the envelope is inside CONTENT_GUARD with a full record's headroom. What
' it costs is drain rate after an outage: a full 250-slot queue takes ten and a
' half minutes to clear rather than five. What it buys is 1.4 KB, and this
' station produces about thirty readings an hour.
Const POST_EVERY = 30              'seconds between POST attempts
Const MAX_BATCH = 12               'readings per POST

' Retry backoff after a failed POST: doubles from BACKOFF_MIN up to BACKOFF_MAX.
Const BACKOFF_MIN = 30             'seconds
Const BACKOFF_MAX = 900            'seconds -- 15 minutes

' --- publishing over MQTT ----------------------------------------------------
'
' The second path. docs/ingest-mqtt.md is the contract; the broker connection
' itself is NOT configured here -- it is a block of Device Configuration Utility
' settings (broker URL, port, TLS, client id, username, password, clean session,
' and the Last Will), because there is no CRBasic instruction that opens a
' broker session. logger/README.md lists every one of them.
'
' Set this False on a base station with no broker credential. The HTTP half is
' unaffected, and since v2.1 it costs nothing else either -- see the note at
' QueuePush about what QDropped used to do here.
Const MQTT_ENABLE = True

' The <station> segment of the topic, and it is the BUREAU STATION NUMBER for a
' site that has one. A base station never does -- it is an ingest point, not a
' gauging station -- so it is the station id. Change this and the broker's ACL
' together: the ACL is generated from the same column, so a mistyped segment is
' refused by the broker rather than filed under an identity nobody claims.
Const MQTT_STATION = "18_bateson"
Const MQTT_DEVICE = "logger"

Const MQTT_QOS = 1                 'at least once. The bridge deduplicates, so
                                   'the redelivery QoS 1 can cause is free
Const MQTT_KEEP = 1                'retained -- the status topic
Const MQTT_DROP = 0                'not retained -- readings

Const MQTT_EVERY = 30              'seconds between publish attempts
Const MQTT_BACKOFF_MIN = 30        'seconds
Const MQTT_BACKOFF_MAX = 900       'seconds -- 15 minutes

' How often to re-publish the retained status message. Re-published on a timer
' rather than only at startup because a logger that reconnects after an outage
' has to overwrite the will the broker published for it, and there is no connect
' event in CRBasic to hang that on.
Const MQTT_STATUS_EVERY = 900      'seconds

' --- local sensors, wired to this logger's own terminals ---------------------
'
' docs/ingest-http.md carries the second address shape for these: a
' `station_number` plus a `channel` saying which sensor at that number spoke. So
' a local reading posts as
'
'   {"station_number":"999998","channel":"rain","reading_ts":"..",
'    "value_raw":3,"value":0.600,"unit":"mm","protocol":"wired"}
'
' and lands on meganet.reading as addr `s:999998/rain`, beside `a:6270` off the
' air -- one queue, one batch, one POST, two address shapes.
'
' LOCAL_NUMBER is the station number those channels report under. 999998 is
' deliberately outside the Bureau's range -- real numbers are five or six digits
' and none of them is 999998 -- so a workshop rig cannot collide with a gauging
' station and says as much on sight. **It has to match the station row created
' by db/migrations/0026_bateson_test_rig.sql.** If it does not, the readings are
' still stored, but they resolve to nobody: the app shows a bare address where a
' station name should be, which is the symptom to recognise.
Const LOCAL_ENABLE = True
Const LOCAL_NUMBER = "999998"

' The wire protocol behind a local reading, recorded against every one of them
' the way PROTOCOL is for the radio. There is no wire and no protocol -- the
' sensor is on a terminal strip -- and that is a fact worth recording rather than
' leaving as the batch envelope's "alert2", which would be untrue of every local
' reading in it.
'
' Added as protocol code 6 by db/migrations/0026_bateson_test_rig.sql, which is
' applied to the live project. Against a database WITHOUT it, code_for() raises
' on a protocol key it does not know and the row loop catches that per reading,
' so every local reading comes back in `rejected` saying `unknown protocol:
' wired` while the radio readings in the same batch are stored. LastReject in
' the Public table is where that appears.
Const LOCAL_PROTOCOL = "wired"

' Minutes between local readings. Every channel reports on this interval, and
' the rain gauge's tips are totalised across it -- so this is the rainfall
' accumulation period as well as the reporting one.
Const LOCAL_EVERY = 5              'minutes

' How many local channels there are, and which is which. The names are the
' `channel` values on the wire, so they are also what the Message Log shows and
' what a query filters on -- change one and the readings before and after the
' change are two different addresses.
Const LOC_N = 4
Const LOC_RAIN = 1
Const LOC_LEVEL1 = 2
Const LOC_LEVEL2 = 3
Const LOC_BATT = 4

' The tipping bucket. PulseCount's channel on a CR300 is the P_SW terminal;
' config 2 is switch closure, option 0 is counts. On a CR1000X or CR6 this is a
' C terminal configured for pulse input -- see "Other dataloggers" at the foot.
Const LOCAL_RAIN_CHAN = P_SW
Const RAIN_CONFIG = 2              'switch closure
Const RAIN_OPTION = 0              'counts, not a rate

' The SDI-12 bus. One port, one address per sensor, "M!" for a standard measure.
' The two addresses below are the wiring as it stands at 18 Bateson -- level 1
' answers on address 1 and level 2 on address 0. Check them with a terminal
' (?! returns every address on the bus) before changing anything here.
Const LOCAL_SDI_PORT = C1
Const LVL1_SDI_ADDR = 1
Const LVL2_SDI_ADDR = 0

' A sanity ceiling on a local engineering value, in whatever unit the channel
' reports. A disconnected SDI-12 sensor returns NAN, which is caught on its own;
' a shorted or mis-scaled one returns a number, and a number is what gets stored
' forever. Anything beyond this is counted at LocalRejected and not queued.
Const LOCAL_MAX = 10000

' --- the live end-to-end self-test -------------------------------------------
'
' See the note in the header. TestInject builds the BINARY frame this receiver
' actually emits, so the decoder under test is the decoder in use.
Const TEST_ENABLE = True

' --- clock -------------------------------------------------------------------
'
' NTPOffset is seconds of offset from UTC, so 0 means the logger's clock runs in
' UTC -- which is what lets the timestamps below end in Z and mean it. Blank
' NTP_SERVER to turn this off and set the clock by hand.
Const NTP_SERVER = "au.pool.ntp.org"
Const NTP_EVERY_HR = 6

' Refuse to stamp a reading until NTP has succeeded at least once.
'
' This exists because of a real failure, not a hypothetical one. A logger whose
' clock was left on local time stamped every reading with the right digits and
' the wrong instant -- readings landed ten hours in the future, the endpoint
' accepted them (its ceiling is a day), and they were invisible in the app
' because every time window there ends at now. Nothing downstream can detect
' that, and nothing can undo it afterwards.
Const REQUIRE_NTP = True

' The clock sanity window, and it is now a window rather than a floor.
'
' MIN_YEAR is the old floor: the endpoint rejects anything before 1990 as a dead
' clock, and this refuses to stamp one in the first place. Set above 1990 on
' purpose -- a logger whose clock has reset can land just after 1990 and pass a
' literal 1990 test while still being meaningless.
'
' MAX_YEAR is new at v3.0 and is a consequence of how a timestamp is now stored:
' one Long holding the calendar fields packed together, which is what took 6.25
' KB out of the ring buffer (see section 3). MAX_YEAR is the last year that is
' WHOLLY representable in a 32-bit signed Long, which is the only useful
' definition of the limit: the last second of 2085 packs to 2,121,292,799
' against a ceiling of 2,147,483,647, and 2086 STARTS inside the range and
' overflows part-way through it -- so a MAX_YEAR of 2086 would accept January
' and wrap silently in December. StampNow refuses past 2085 instead of wrapping
' and stamping every reading in MIN_YEAR, which nothing downstream could
' detect. `npm run logger` asserts both sides of that edge.
Const MIN_YEAR = 2020
Const MAX_YEAR = 2085

' --- sizes -------------------------------------------------------------------

' How much unsent data to hold. Each slot is one reading. This station hears
' about thirty readings an hour, so 250 slots is roughly eight hours -- it was
' described as two when the number was guessed rather than measured.
Const Q_SIZE = 250

' The JSON body buffer, and the length at which BuildBatch stops adding. Raise
' both together if you raise MAX_BATCH, and leave at least 180 characters -- one
' longest-case local reading -- between the guard and the buffer, or the last
' append can still overrun.
Const CONTENT_SIZE = 1600
Const CONTENT_GUARD = 1400

' A reading whose 11-bit value is all ones. alert2.js calls this out as
' over-range or a dead sensor, so it is posted with quality "suspect" rather
' than dropped -- a stuck sensor is a fact about the network, not a bad row.
Const FULL_SCALE = 2047

' The ALERT2 ASCII protocol's fixed fields, before the payload hex begins, and
' the most fields this program will split a line into.
Const N_FIELDS = 24
Const FIELD_MAX = 60

' Payload byte 0 of an ALERT2 "ALERT concentration" element. Decimal 116, which
' is 0x74 -- written in decimal so there is no doubt what the compiler read.
Const ELEM_CONCENTRATION = 116

' --- the ERT-A2's BINARY framing ---------------------------------------------
'
' The receiver on this base station does not emit the ALERT2 ASCII lines. It
' emits a binary frame, and a terminal capture of three of them settles the
' layout completely:
'
'   41 4C 45 52 54 32 | 56 | 9C 2C 02 00 01 75 ... 84 01 0B 74 5C 9E 87 09 8C 00
'   |___ "ALERT2" ___|  ^                          |_ anchor _||__ element ____|
'                       byte 7 is a LENGTH, not the "A" of ALERT2A
'
'   total bytes = 6 + byte 7.  0x56 -> 92 bytes, 0x52 -> 88 bytes, both exact.
'
' Byte 7 read as text is "V" or "R", which is why a terminal shows ALERT2V and
' ALERT2R and why a program looking for the literal text ALERT2A never matched
' one. The ASCII form is still recognised -- see the mode test in TakeFrames --
' because "ALERT2A," is a letter A followed by a comma and a length byte cannot
' be.
'
' Inside the frame, the ALERT concentration element sits behind a fixed anchor:
'
'   84 01 <len> 74 <time hi> <time lo> then <len-3>/4 four-byte records
Const SIG_LEN = 6                  'the length of "ALERT2"
Const BUF_MAX = 400                'the byte buffer, in bytes. One longest frame
                                   'and a third, which is what a 1-second scan
                                   'can see. Was 600
Const FRAME_MAX = 300              'a length byte claiming more than this is not
                                   'a length byte
Const FRAME_MIN_LEN = 12           'nor is one claiming less than this
Const FRAME_LOOPS = 24             'most frames or lines taken from the buffer in
                                   'one scan


'==============================================================================
' 2. DIAGNOSTICS -- what a technician reads in the Public table
'==============================================================================
'
' Everything below is visible in LoggerNet, PC400 or Device Configuration
' Utility without collecting a table.
'
' This section is a third of what it was, and the rule used for cutting it was:
' **keep everything that says what the program is doing NOW, delete everything
' that was working out what the feed IS.** The second question is answered --
' see the version note at the top of the file.

'--- which ingest point this is ----------------------------------------------
'
' Lands in every reading's `path` column and is what the Message Log tab shows
' as "which base heard it". Public rather than Const, so it can be corrected at
' the logger without a recompile; the value here is the default at every program
' start, not a saved setting.
Public BASE_NAME As String * 20 = "18 Bateson"

'--- is the logger alive ------------------------------------------------------
Public Batt_volt                   'V -- station supply
Public PTemp                       'degC -- panel temperature
Public ClockOK As Boolean          'false = clock failed its sanity check, and
                                   'nothing is being stamped or queued
Public NowISO As String * 24       'the clock, formatted exactly as it is posted
Public NowWhen As Long             'and the same instant packed into one Long --
                                   'see PackWhen. This is what a queue slot
                                   'holds, and the two being different views of
                                   'one clock is checkable here by eye
Public NTPErrMs                    'clock error NTP found, in ms, before it
                                   'corrected it. NAN = the sync failed
Public NTPLastOK As Boolean
Public ClockVerified As Boolean    'NTP has succeeded at least once since start
Public ClockState As String * 60   'plain English: what the clock is doing
Public SecsSinceNTP
Public WatchdogErrs                'non-zero means the logger has been resetting
Public SkippedScans                'the main scan overrunning its second
Public VarOutOfBound               'an array index that went past its dimension

'--- is the receiver talking --------------------------------------------------
Public SecsSinceRx                 'seconds since the last byte arrived. THE
                                   'first number to read when nothing is coming
                                   'through: it separates "the receiver is
                                   'silent" from "this program cannot parse it"
Public RxAvail                     'bytes waiting in the port buffer
Public RxBlockBytes                'bytes SerialInBlock returned this scan
Public RxBytesTotal                'bytes read since startup
Public RxNulls                     'NUL bytes seen. Non-zero on an ASCII feed
                                   'means the port format is wrong
Public RxBufLen                    'bytes waiting to be framed
Public RxBufDrops                  'times the buffer filled with nothing framable
Public RxLines                     'complete TEXT lines cut out since startup
Public RxNonText                   'runs discarded as not-text -- binary that
                                   'reached a CR or LF before it was framed
Public RxFrameMode As String * 8   'ascii or binary, decided per frame
Public RxTermCR                    'text lines ended by a bare CR
Public RxTermLF                    'and by LF -- CRLF counts here, and the
                                   'CR before it is stripped as whitespace

'--- what the last frame decoded to -------------------------------------------
'
' The pipeline as it now stands: bytes -> frame -> element -> record -> reading.
' Where this display stops being what you expect is the step that is broken.
Public RxStep As String * 40       'the last step the pipeline completed
Public RxHex As String * 124       'the first 40 bytes of the last frame, in hex.
                                   'The ONE hex display left, and it is the one
                                   'that answers "what actually arrived" -- the
                                   'other nine were the same question asked of
                                   'the block, the buffer, the line, the bad
                                   'line and the element separately
Public FrLenByte                   'byte 7, the length
Public FrTotal                     '6 + FrLenByte, the whole frame
Public FrElemVia                   'how the concentration element was found:
                                   '1 the 84 01 <len> 74 anchor, 2 the loose
                                   'scan, 0 neither -- read this first when a
                                   'frame produces no readings
Public FrElemOff                   'where it starts, from the frame start
Public FrElemLen                   'its length byte
Public FrTimeSecs                  'the two time bytes inside it
Public FrRecords                   'four-byte records it carries
Public FrGood                      'of those, how many decoded
Public FrBad                       'and how many had a non-zero status byte or
                                   'an implausible address

Public RxLastLine As String * 250  'the last complete TEXT line, verbatim
Public RxLastShape As String * 16  'what it was taken to be: ALERT2A, plain, or
                                   'unrecognised
Public RxLastFields                'fields the splitter found in it
Public RxLastRecHex As String * 12 'the four payload bytes the last reading came
                                   'out of, so the bit-unpacking can be checked
                                   'by hand against the table at ParseAlert2
Public RxLastId As Long            'ALERT id decoded from them
Public RxLastValue As Long         'and the 11-bit value
Public RxLastSuspect As Boolean    'value was full scale
Public RxLastJson As String * 120  'the JSON object for that reading, byte for
                                   'byte as it will appear in the batch
Public RxLastWhy As String * 60    'why the last rejected frame or line was
Public RxLastBad As String * 120   'and the first 120 characters of it
Public whyCode As Long             'the same reason as a number -- see the list
                                   'at RejectLine

Public RxFrames                    'frames that decoded
Public RxReadings                  'readings extracted from them
Public RxBadFrames                 'frames or lines rejected
Public RxBadRecords                'records inside good frames whose status byte
                                   'was non-zero or whose address was impossible
Public RxFrameSkew                 'seconds between the frame's own ALERT2 time
                                   'and this logger's clock. Near zero means the
                                   'two agree and the frame time is UTC; parked
                                   'at a whole number of hours is a finding

'--- the local sensors on this logger's terminals -----------------------------
'
' The channel table is Public rather than Const so the names, units and decimal
' places are readable at the logger beside the values they describe -- checking
' MegaNet's station row against what this logger actually sends is then one
' screen rather than two documents. Filled in BeginProg.
Public LocName(LOC_N) As String * 12   'the `channel` on the wire
Public LocUnit(LOC_N) As String * 8    'the `unit` beside `value`
Public LocRawDp(LOC_N) As Long         'decimals for value_raw: 0 for counts

Public Rain_tips                   'tips PulseCount reported this scan. Almost
                                   'always 0 -- it is the accumulator that
                                   'matters
Public Rain_bucket = 0.2           'mm per tip. The number written on the gauge.
                                   'Public so it can be corrected at the logger,
                                   'and it travels with the reading in
                                   '`conversion` so a change does not rewrite
                                   'what was already stored
Public Rain_tips_int As Long       'tips so far this reporting interval
Public Rain_mm                     'and those tips in mm, so far this interval
Public Rain_last_mm                'the last completed interval's total, which
                                   'is the number that was actually sent
Public Rain_total_mm               'since this program started. Not sent -- it
                                   'is here so a bench test can be checked
                                   'against a measuring cylinder

Public Level1_m                    'SDI-12 address LVL1_SDI_ADDR
Public Level2_m                    'SDI-12 address LVL2_SDI_ADDR
                                   'NAN means the sensor did not answer, which
                                   'is caught before queueing -- see
                                   'LocalRejected

Public LocalState As String * 60   'plain English: what the local half is doing
Public LocalChan As String * 12    'the channel of the last local reading queued
Public LocalRaw                    'its value_raw
Public LocalEng                    'and its value
Public LocalQueued As Long         'local readings queued since startup
Public LocalRejected As Long       'local reads refused: NAN, or past LOCAL_MAX.
                                   'Non-zero means a sensor is not answering or
                                   'is not scaled the way this program thinks
Public SecsSinceLocal              'seconds since the last one was queued

'--- the live end-to-end self-test --------------------------------------------
Public TestFire As Boolean         'set true to fire one. Clears itself
Public TestId As Long = 8101       'the ALERT address to transmit as. Must be
                                   '1-8191, because the wire carries 13 bits of
                                   'address and nothing wider fits -- which is
                                   'why this is 8101 and not a 9000-block
                                   'address like the ELPRO bench unit's. The
                                   'highest real address in the registry is
                                   '7999, so 8100-8109 is free, recognisably
                                   'synthetic AND transmittable. 8101 is the
                                   'address MegaNet's station row reserves
Public TestValue As Long = 21      'and the value, 0-2047 -- 11 bits. Change it
                                   'between shots so the second one is a new
                                   'reading rather than a duplicate
Public TestEvery As Long = 0       'minutes between automatic shots. 0 = off, and
                                   'off is the shipped default: an unattended
                                   'self-test writes synthetic readings into the
                                   'live database forever
Public TestFired As Long           'shots fired since startup
Public TestState As String * 60    'plain English: fired, waiting, or refused
Public TestHex As String * 60      'the frame it built, in hex. Compare it
                                   'against RxHex: they must be the same bytes,
                                   'because they are

'--- is the queue draining ----------------------------------------------------
'
' One ring buffer, two readers. The pair being far apart is the diagnostic: it
' says one path is down and which one, at a glance, before anything else.
Public QDepth                      'readings the buffer is still holding for at
                                   'least one of the two paths
Public QDepthHttp                  'readings waiting to be POSTed
Public QDepthMqtt                  'readings waiting to be published
Public QPeak                       'the deepest it has ever been
Public QDropped                    'readings lost to a full queue. Non-zero means
                                   'a path that something reads has been down
                                   'longer than the buffer holds -- see the note
                                   'at QueuePush about what this used to count

'--- did the last POST work ---------------------------------------------------
Public PostState As String * 40    'plain English: what the poster is doing
Public TokenOK As Boolean          'the token file was found and was not empty
Public TokenLen As Long            'how many characters it held -- enough to spot
                                   'a truncated paste without printing the secret
Public PostAttempts
Public PostOK
Public PostFail
Public ConsecFail                  'consecutive failures -- resets on success
Public Backoff                     'seconds until the next attempt, when failing
Public SecsSincePost               'seconds since the last ACCEPTED post
Public Accepted                    'cumulative, from the endpoint's own answer
Public Duplicates                  'readings it had already stored -- normal
Public Rejected                    'readings it refused. Non-zero needs reading:
                                   'LastReject says why
Public LastAccepted
Public LastDuplicates
Public LastRejected
Public LastStatus As Long          'the HTTP status code: 200 good, 401 token,
                                   '400 body
Public LastSocket                  'HTTPPost's own return: >=100 is the TCP
                                   'socket, 0 is no answer, -2 did not execute
Public LastReject As String * 150  'the first "why" out of the last rejected
                                   'array, or the first of an error body
Public HTTPResponse As String * 300
Public RespHeader As String * 300  'what came back where the request headers
                                   'were -- see PostBatch

'--- did the last PUBLISH work ------------------------------------------------
Public MqttState As String * 40    'plain English: what the publisher is doing
Public MqttTopic As String * 100   'the reading topic this station publishes on
Public MqttStatusTopic As String * 100 'and the status topic, which is also the
                                   'Last Will topic to set in DevConfig
Public MqttAttempts
Public MqttOK
Public MqttFail
Public MqttConsecFail
Public MqttBackoff
Public MqttPublished               'readings handed to the broker
Public MqttResult As Long          'MQTTPublish's own return: 0 is success
Public MqttStatusResult As Long
Public MqttLastBatch As Long
Public MqttStatusBody As String * 100
Public SecsSinceMqtt               'since the last successful publish
Public SecsSinceTry                'since the last ATTEMPT -- the backoff is
                                   'measured from this one
Public SecsSinceStatus

'--- what the last reading archived to Readings was ---------------------------
Public RdAddr As Long              'positive = ALERT address, negative = the
                                   'local channel. One column for both, because
                                   'the two cannot collide: an ALERT address is
                                   '1-65535 and a channel is 1-LOC_N
Public RdValue                     'and its raw value


'==============================================================================
' 3. WORKING VARIABLES
'==============================================================================

' The unsent-readings ring buffer. QHead is the next slot to write; each path
' has its own next-to-send. A path is caught up when its tail equals QHead, so
' capacity is Q_SIZE - 1 for whichever path is furthest behind.
'
' ONE buffer and two tails rather than two buffers, because the readings are the
' same readings. No semaphore guards it, and that is deliberate: the main scan
' owns QHead; the poster sequence owns both tails, and it is a single sequence
' rather than one per path precisely so that stays true. The one crossing is on
' overflow, where QueuePush has to move a tail sitting on the slot it is about
' to reuse -- a window one instruction wide, costing one reading sent twice
' (free, by contract) or one dropped a scan early.
'
' --- THREE columns, where v2.1 had seven, and no slot was lost --------------
'
' v2.1 held qAddr(Long), qVal(Long), qStamp(String*24), qSus(Boolean),
' qChan(Long), qRaw(Float) and qEng(Float): 49 bytes a slot, 12,250 bytes for
' the buffer alone. It is now 12 bytes a slot and 3,000 in total, at the same
' 250 slots, and every one of the four columns that went was derivable:
'
'   qStamp   the timestamp was a 24-character ISO string, 6,250 bytes of the
'            12,250. It is now ONE Long holding the calendar fields packed
'            together -- see PackWhen -- and the ISO string is rebuilt at the
'            moment the reading is rendered into JSON. Not an epoch: an epoch
'            needs leap-year arithmetic in both directions and has a cliff in
'            2038. Packing is exact, reverses by division, and its own cliff is
'            MAX_YEAR, which is stated in section 1 rather than left to be
'            discovered.
'
'   qChan    a slot is a radio reading or a local channel, never both, so the
'            two share one column: qAddr is POSITIVE for an ALERT address and
'            NEGATIVE for a local channel. They cannot collide -- an ALERT
'            address is 1-65535 and a channel is 1-LOC_N.
'
'   qSus     "the value was full scale" is a test on the value, not a fact about
'            the slot: MakeRec asks qVal(mi) = FULL_SCALE and gets the same
'            answer the flag held.
'
'   qEng     the engineering value is the raw one converted, and the conversion
'            is a property of the CHANNEL rather than of the reading -- rain is
'            tips times the bucket, and the other three are already engineering
'            values. MakeRec applies it.
'
' qVal is a Float now rather than a Long, so it carries both an 11-bit radio
' count and a level in metres. IEEE4 holds every integer to 2^24 exactly, so a
' radio value and a tip count are exact in it; MakeRec copies to a Long before
' Sprintf's %d, which reads a Long and would print nonsense from a float.
Dim qAddr(Q_SIZE) As Long          '>0 ALERT address, <0 local channel
Dim qVal(Q_SIZE) As Float          'value_raw, whichever kind
Dim qWhen(Q_SIZE) As Long          'the instant, packed -- see PackWhen
Dim QHead As Long
Dim QTailHttp As Long              'the next slot to POST
Dim QTailMqtt As Long              'the next slot to publish
Dim qLag As Long                   'scratch, for the depth arithmetic in the scan
Dim qLost As Boolean               'QueuePush: this overflow actually cost a
                                   'reading somebody was still waiting for

' Dim, not Public, on purpose: a Public token is readable by anyone who can
' reach the logger, which is the whole thing the token file was for.
Dim IngestToken As String * 80

' One body buffer, shared by both paths. They are built in the same sequence,
' one after the other, and each is handed to its instruction before the next is
' built.
Dim ContentsString As String * CONTENT_SIZE
Dim HTTPHeader As String * 300
Dim BatchN As Long                 'readings in the batch currently in flight
Dim bWrap As Boolean               'BuildBatch's two inputs, set by the caller
Dim bFrom As Long                  'immediately before the call
Dim CRLF As String * 2
Dim DQ As String * 1               'a double quote. CHR(34) is the portable way
                                   'to get one into a CRBasic string
Dim MqttLastPayload As String * 200

' Serial working storage.
Dim RxBuf As String * 256          'what SerialInBlock writes into
Dim rxByte(BUF_MAX) As Long        'the byte buffer. Numeric, so a NUL is just 0
Dim nBuf As Long
Dim RxLine As String * RX_MAX      'one TEXT line, when there is one
Dim dropN As Long                  'BufDrop
Dim dk2 As Long
Dim fk As Long                     'TakeFrames / DecodeFrame
Dim frSigAt As Long
Dim frEnd As Long
Dim frLoops As Long
Dim frElemAt As Long
Dim frRec As Long
Dim tk2 As Long                    'TakeTextLine
Dim tb As Long
Dim tEnd As Long
Dim tOK As Boolean
Dim tTook As Boolean               'TakeTextLine consumed something
Dim frDone As Boolean              'TakeFrames: this pass made no progress
Dim frIsAscii As Boolean
Dim hx As Long                     'HexDump
Dim hxFrom As Long
Dim hxN As Long
Dim hxNum As String * 4

Dim fld(FIELD_MAX) As String * 12
Dim nFld As Long
Dim pay(32) As Long                'payload bytes, decoded from hex
Dim nPay As Long
Dim frameOk As Long
Dim payLen As Long
Dim frameSecs As Long
Dim loggerSecs As Long
Dim suspect As Long

Dim rTime(9) As Long               '1 yr, 2 mo, 3 day, 4 hr, 5 min, 6 sec,
                                   '7 usec, 8 day-of-week, 9 day-of-year

' JSON fragments, built once in BeginProg so the per-reading concatenation in
' MakeRec stays legible instead of becoming a wall of CHR(34). A tilde stands
' for a double quote in the comments below, so the comments carry no quote
' characters of their own and CRBasic Editor's syntax colouring survives them.
Dim jEnvelope As String * 200      '~path~:~..~,~protocol~:~..~,~readings~:[
Dim jTail As String * 8            ']}} -- closes the HTTP payload wrapper too
Dim jTailBare As String * 8        ']} -- the MQTT one, which has no wrapper
Dim fId As String * 16             '{~alert_id~:
Dim fTs As String * 20             ',~reading_ts~:~
Dim fVal As String * 20            '~,~value_raw~:
Dim fSus As String * 24            ',~quality~:~suspect~
Dim fSn As String * 28             '{~station_number~:~   -- opens the quote
Dim fCh As String * 24             '~,~channel~:~         -- closes it, opens
                                   'the channel name, which the caller closes
Dim fEng As String * 12            ',~value~:
Dim fUnit As String * 12           ',~unit~:~
Dim fConv As String * 20           ',~conversion~:~
Dim fProto As String * 32          ',~protocol~:~wired~ -- fixed, so it is built
                                   'whole rather than assembled per reading
Dim jName As String * 24           'BASE_NAME, safe to put inside a JSON literal
Dim nk2 As Long
Dim nc2 As Long
Dim jRec As String * 200           'one reading, being built

' Scratch, one set per subroutine, so a call never quietly clobbers its caller's
' loop counter. CRBasic has no local scope worth relying on here.
Dim si As Long                     'SplitLine
Dim sStart As Long
Dim sChar As String * 1
Dim delim As String * 1
Dim jp As Long                     'JsonInt
Dim jd As Long
Dim jsonKey As String * 16
Dim jsonVal As Long
Dim pp As Long                     'ParseStatus
Dim pd As Long
Dim hk As Long                     'HexByte
Dim hn As Long
Dim hexVal As Long
Dim hexIn As String * 4
Dim fi As Long                     'FindStr
Dim srcStr As String * 300
Dim needle As String * 32
Dim foundAt As Long
Dim numIn As String * 16           'IsNum
Dim numOK As Boolean
Dim nk As Long
Dim nc As Long
Dim fh As Long                     'LoadToken -- the file handle
Dim ltk As Long                    'and its trimming counter. Separate from the
                                   'main scan's on purpose: LoadToken runs in the
                                   'POSTER sequence, and a shared counter between
                                   'two tasks is a mis-trimmed token that fails
                                   'to authenticate once in a blue moon and never
                                   'reproduces
Dim tk As Long                     'ParseLine trimming -- main scan only
Dim ak As Long                     'ParseLine's own search for ALERT2A. Not
Dim aFound As Long                 'FindStr, because FindStr's scratch belongs
                                   'to the poster, which parses the HTTP
                                   'response with it
Dim i As Long                      'ParseAlert2, and nothing else: the scan that
                                   'calls it must not use i for its own counter
Dim bi As Long                     'BuildBatch
Dim mi As Long                     'MakeRec -- the queue slot being rendered
Dim pNum As String * 16            'the poster's number scratch
Dim pLong As Long                  'and its Long, for Sprintf's %d
Dim pStamp As String * 24          'and the ISO string it rebuilds per reading
Dim mNum As String * 16            'the publisher's number scratch. A second
                                   'one, because it is written while
                                   'ContentsString is being handed to an
                                   'instruction, and sharing it with the poster
                                   'is a status message eventually built out of
                                   'half a reading
Dim dNum As String * 16            'the main scan's number scratch
Dim postDue As Boolean
Dim mqttWait As Long               'seconds the broker is to be left alone for
Dim mqttSent As Boolean            'this tick's publish actually went out
Dim statusDue As Boolean

Dim pushChan As Long               'QueuePush's input: 0 for a radio reading,
Dim pushRaw As Float               '1..LOC_N for a local channel, with its raw
                                   'value here. Set by the caller immediately
                                   'before the call -- the same arrangement
                                   'bWrap/bFrom use
Dim locPend(LOC_N) As Boolean      'a staged local reading waiting for the main
Dim locRawV(LOC_N) As Float        'scan to queue it -- see LocalPush for why
                                   'the handover is a flag and not a queue
Dim lk As Long                     'LocalPush
Dim qn As Long                     'QueuePush
Dim uv As Long                     'UnWhen -- the packed instant being unpacked
Dim uY As Long                     'and the six fields it comes back as
Dim uMo As Long
Dim uD As Long
Dim uH As Long
Dim uMi As Long
Dim uS As Long
Dim tstK As Long                   'TestInject
Dim tstSecs As Long                'Long, not the default float: the packing and
Dim tstB0 As Long                  'the hex display both read Longs
Dim tstB1 As Long
Dim tstB2 As Long
Dim tstFrame(18) As Long           'the synthetic frame, byte by byte


'==============================================================================
' 4. DATA TABLES
'==============================================================================

' The local archive, and the reason a broken link is an inconvenience rather
' than a data loss. Every reading is stored here whether or not it ever posts,
' so a base station that spent a fortnight offline can still be collected over
' a cable and backfilled.
'
' Two columns, and they carry both kinds of reading: RdAddr is the ALERT address
' for a radio reading and the NEGATED channel number for a local one, the same
' convention the ring buffer uses. RxFrameSkew came out of here at v3.0 -- it is
' a property of the receiver rather than of a reading, it is in Diag every five
' minutes, and it was a third of this table's width.
'
' Sized, not auto-allocated. Campbell costs an auto-allocated event-driven table
' at one record per second, so a -1 here would claim almost all the memory and
' starve the heartbeat below.
DataTable (Readings,True,20000)
  Sample (1,RdAddr,Long)
  Sample (1,RdValue,IEEE4)
EndTable

' The heartbeat. Five minutes is frequent enough to see a link flapping and slow
' enough to keep months on a CR300. It carries BOTH paths' results as well as
' the counters, which is what answers "when did it stop working, and which half
' of it".
'
' Counters are Long, not FP2: FP2 carries four significant digits and cannot
' hold an integer above 7999 exactly, so a POST counter stored as FP2 starts
' lying on about the fourth day.
DataTable (Diag,True,-1)
  DataInterval (0,5,Min,10)
  Minimum (1,Batt_volt,FP2,False,False)
  Sample (1,PTemp,FP2)
  Sample (1,WatchdogErrs,Long)
  Sample (1,SkippedScans,Long)
  Sample (1,VarOutOfBound,Long)
  Sample (1,NTPErrMs,FP2)
  Sample (1,RxBytesTotal,Long)
  Sample (1,RxNulls,Long)
  Sample (1,RxLines,Long)
  Sample (1,RxFrames,Long)
  Sample (1,RxReadings,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBadFrames,Long)
  Sample (1,RxBadRecords,Long)
  Sample (1,SecsSinceRx,Long)
  Sample (1,RxFrameSkew,IEEE4)
  Sample (1,QDepth,Long)
  Sample (1,QDepthHttp,Long)
  Sample (1,QDepthMqtt,Long)
  Sample (1,QPeak,Long)
  Sample (1,QDropped,Long)
  Sample (1,LocalQueued,Long)
  Sample (1,LocalRejected,Long)
  Sample (1,Rain_total_mm,FP2)
  Sample (1,Level1_m,IEEE4)
  Sample (1,Level2_m,IEEE4)
  Sample (1,TestFired,Long)
  Sample (1,Accepted,Long)
  Sample (1,Duplicates,Long)
  Sample (1,Rejected,Long)
  Sample (1,PostOK,Long)
  Sample (1,PostFail,Long)
  Sample (1,LastStatus,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttOK,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttFail,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttPublished,Long)
  Sample (1,MqttResult,Long)
  Sample (1,PostState,String)
  Sample (1,MqttState,String)
  Sample (1,ClockState,String)
  Sample (1,LastReject,String)
EndTable


'==============================================================================
' 5. SUBROUTINES
'==============================================================================
'
' Declared before BeginProg, and in call order. They write their answers into
' module-level variables rather than returning them: it reads a little flatter
' than a Function would, and it sidesteps every question about how CRBasic
' passes strings and arrays into a call.


Sub HexByte()
  hexVal = 0
  If Len(hexIn) = 0 Then
    hexVal = -1
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  For hk = 1 To Len(hexIn)
    hn = ASCII(Mid(hexIn,hk,1))
    If hn >= 48 AND hn <= 57 Then
      hn = hn - 48                               '0-9
    ElseIf hn >= 65 AND hn <= 70 Then
      hn = hn - 55                               'A-F
    ElseIf hn >= 97 AND hn <= 102 Then
      hn = hn - 87                               'a-f
    Else
      hexVal = -1
      ExitSub
    EndIf
    hexVal = hexVal * 16 + hn
  Next hk
EndSub

Sub FindStr()
  foundAt = 0
  If Len(needle) = 0 Then ExitSub
  If Len(srcStr) < Len(needle) Then ExitSub
  For fi = 1 To Len(srcStr) - Len(needle) + 1
    If Mid(srcStr,fi,Len(needle)) = needle Then
      foundAt = fi
      ExitFor
    EndIf
  Next fi
EndSub

Sub SplitLine()
  nFld = 0
  sStart = 1
  For si = 1 To Len(RxLine) + 1
    If si > Len(RxLine) Then
      sChar = delim                              'flush the final field
    Else
      sChar = Mid(RxLine,si,1)
    EndIf
    If sChar = delim Then
      nFld = nFld + 1
      If nFld <= FIELD_MAX Then fld(nFld) = Mid(RxLine,sStart,si - sStart)
      sStart = si + 1
    EndIf
  Next si
  If nFld > FIELD_MAX Then nFld = FIELD_MAX
EndSub

Sub IsNum()
  numOK = False
  If Len(numIn) = 0 Then ExitSub
  For nk = 1 To Len(numIn)
    nc = ASCII(Mid(numIn,nk,1))
    If nk = 1 AND nc = 45 AND Len(numIn) > 1 Then
      'a leading minus is allowed
    ElseIf nc < 48 OR nc > 57 Then
      ExitSub
    EndIf
  Next nk
  numOK = True
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' HexDump -- hxN bytes of the byte buffer from hxFrom, as padded hex, into RxHex.
'
' What is left of the nine hex displays v2.1 carried. One of them, over the
' bytes that actually became a frame, is the answer to "what arrived" -- the
' other eight asked the same question of the block, the buffer, the line and the
' bad line separately, which mattered only while nobody knew which of those the
' feed was made of.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub HexDump()
  RxHex = ""
  For hx = hxFrom To hxFrom + hxN - 1
    If hx > nBuf Then ExitFor
    If Len(RxHex) > 118 Then
      RxHex = RxHex & "..."
      ExitFor
    EndIf
    Sprintf (hxNum,"%02X ",rxByte(hx))
    RxHex = RxHex & hxNum
  Next hx
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PackWhen / UnWhen -- one instant as one Long, and back.
'
' This is what took 6,250 bytes out of the ring buffer. A slot used to hold the
' timestamp as a 24-character ISO string; it now holds this number, and MakeRec
' rebuilds the string at the moment the reading is rendered.
'
' The packing is positional, in the order a date is written:
'
'   v = year - MIN_YEAR
'   v = v * 12 + (month - 1)
'   v = v * 31 + (day - 1)          <- 31, always. February the 31st is simply a
'   v = v * 24 + hour                  value this encoding can represent and a
'   v = v * 60 + minute                clock will never produce
'   v = v * 60 + second
'
' and unpacking is the same divisions in reverse. **No calendar arithmetic in
' either direction** -- that is the whole reason for choosing this over seconds
' since an epoch, which needs leap-year handling both ways and puts a cliff at
' 2038 that nothing in the program would announce. The cliff here is MAX_YEAR,
' and StampNow refuses past it rather than wrapping: the largest value this
' produces is the last second of 2085 -- 65*12+11 -> 791, *31+30 -> 24,551,
' *24+23 -> 589,247, *60+59 -> 35,354,879, *60+59 -> 2,121,292,799, against a
' signed Long's 2,147,483,647. The first second of 2086 does not fit.
'
' PackWhen is inline in StampNow rather than a Sub of its own, because it runs
' once per scan and a Sub call to set one variable is a call for nothing.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub UnWhen()
  uS = uv MOD 60
  uv = INT(uv / 60)
  uMi = uv MOD 60
  uv = INT(uv / 60)
  uH = uv MOD 24
  uv = INT(uv / 24)
  uD = (uv MOD 31) + 1
  uv = INT(uv / 31)
  uMo = (uv MOD 12) + 1
  uv = INT(uv / 12)
  uY = uv + MIN_YEAR
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' StampNow -- the clock, into NowISO and NowWhen.
'
' ClockOK is set here and nowhere else: a reading is only queued when it is
' true, because the endpoint's most common rejection is a reading stamped 1970
' by an RTC that lost its backup battery, and a base station that posts a
' thousand of those has made a battery fault look like a network fault.
'
' Sprintf rather than FormatFloat because the zero-padded widths are the whole
' point and Sprintf takes integer specifiers natively -- RealTime fills a Long
' array, so %d matches its arguments exactly.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub StampNow()
  RealTime (rTime())
  If rTime(1) < MIN_YEAR Then
    ClockOK = False
    ClockState = "clock is before " & MIN_YEAR & " - check the RTC battery"
    NowISO = ""
    NowWhen = 0
  ElseIf rTime(1) > MAX_YEAR Then
    'See PackWhen. Refusing is the honest end of a 64-year encoding; wrapping
    'would stamp every reading in MIN_YEAR and nothing downstream could tell.
    ClockOK = False
    ClockState = "clock is past " & MAX_YEAR & " - see PackWhen"
    NowISO = ""
    NowWhen = 0
  ElseIf REQUIRE_NTP AND Len(NTP_SERVER) > 0 AND NOT ClockVerified Then
    'The year is plausible, which proves nothing about the zone. See REQUIRE_NTP.
    ClockOK = False
    ClockState = "waiting for the first NTP sync - not stamping yet"
    NowISO = ""
    NowWhen = 0
  Else
    ClockOK = True
    ClockState = "ok"
    Sprintf (NowISO,"%04d-%02d-%02dT%02d:%02d:%02dZ",rTime(1),rTime(2),rTime(3),rTime(4),rTime(5),rTime(6))
    NowWhen = rTime(1) - MIN_YEAR
    NowWhen = NowWhen * 12 + (rTime(2) - 1)
    NowWhen = NowWhen * 31 + (rTime(3) - 1)
    NowWhen = NowWhen * 24 + rTime(4)
    NowWhen = NowWhen * 60 + rTime(5)
    NowWhen = NowWhen * 60 + rTime(6)
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' MakeRec -- queue slot `mi` rendered as one JSON object, into jRec.
'
' Called only from BuildBatch, which is to say only from the poster sequence.
' QueuePush renders the radio object for RxLastJson with its own copy of six of
' these lines rather than calling this, because the two run in different tasks
' and sharing jRec, mi and a scratch string between them would mean a POST body
' occasionally built out of a reading the main scan was mid-way through
' displaying. **Those six lines must be kept identical** -- that is a cost of
' the split, and a cheaper one than a wrong value_raw nobody can reproduce.
'
' Four things are DERIVED here rather than stored per slot, which is where 5 KB
' of the ring buffer went. See the note at the declaration:
'
'   the ISO stamp   rebuilt from the packed Long
'   radio vs local  the sign of qAddr
'   suspect         the value being full scale
'   the engineering value and its conversion, which are properties of the
'                   channel rather than of the reading
'
' Numbers go through Sprintf and FormatFloat rather than being concatenated
' directly. Implicit numeric-to-string conversion is free to pad, round or reach
' for scientific notation, and any of the three inside a JSON literal is a
' rejected batch. Sprintf's %d reads a Long, so a radio value is copied into one
' first; FormatFloat takes the float.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub MakeRec()
  uv = qWhen(mi)
  Call UnWhen()
  Sprintf (pStamp,"%04d-%02d-%02dT%02d:%02d:%02dZ",uY,uMo,uD,uH,uMi,uS)

  If qAddr(mi) > 0 Then
    'A radio reading: an ALERT address and the count that came off the air.
    Sprintf (pNum,"%d",qAddr(mi))
    jRec = fId & pNum
    jRec = jRec & fTs & pStamp
    pLong = qVal(mi)
    Sprintf (pNum,"%d",pLong)
    jRec = jRec & fVal & pNum
    If pLong = FULL_SCALE Then jRec = jRec & fSus
    jRec = jRec & "}"
  Else
    'A local reading: this station's number, the channel that spoke, and the
    'conversion beside the raw rather than instead of it.
    '
    'NAN cannot reach here: LocalPush refuses to queue one, because
    'FormatFloat(NAN) is the word "nan", which is not JSON, and one of those in
    'a batch is a 400 for every reading in it -- including the radio readings
    'from the same station that were fine.
    lk = -qAddr(mi)
    jRec = fSn & LOCAL_NUMBER & fCh & LocName(lk) & DQ
    jRec = jRec & fTs & pStamp
    If LocRawDp(lk) = 0 Then
      jRec = jRec & fVal & FormatFloat (qVal(mi),"%.0f")
    Else
      jRec = jRec & fVal & FormatFloat (qVal(mi),"%.3f")
    EndIf

    'The conversion, applied here because it belongs to the channel. Rain is the
    'only one that has one: the other three report the number the sensor gave,
    'which is already the engineering value, and a `conversion` saying "none"
    'would be a column of noise on every row.
    If lk = LOC_RAIN Then
      jRec = jRec & fEng & FormatFloat (qVal(mi) * Rain_bucket,"%.3f")
    Else
      jRec = jRec & fEng & FormatFloat (qVal(mi),"%.3f")
    EndIf
    jRec = jRec & fUnit & LocUnit(lk) & DQ
    If lk = LOC_RAIN Then
      jRec = jRec & fConv & "raw x " & FormatFloat (Rain_bucket,"%.3f") & " mm per tip" & DQ
    EndIf
    jRec = jRec & fProto
    jRec = jRec & "}"
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' QueuePush -- one reading into the ring buffer.
'
' A RADIO reading: pushChan = 0, address in RxLastId, value in RxLastValue.
' A LOCAL reading: pushChan = the channel, raw value in pushRaw.
'
' Called from the main scan, which is the only writer of QHead. **That is why
' the local sensors do not queue from the sequence that measures them** -- see
' LocalPush. The poster sequence advances both tails as it delivers; this is the
' one place outside it that touches them, and only when the buffer is full.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub QueuePush()
  qn = QHead + 1
  If qn > Q_SIZE Then qn = 1
  qLost = False
  If qn = QTailHttp OR qn = QTailMqtt Then
    'Full for whichever path is furthest behind. Drop the oldest rather than
    'refuse the newest -- at a flood warning base the current river level is the
    'reading that matters.
    '
    'Every tail sitting on the dropped slot moves, and only those: if HTTP has
    'already sent this reading and MQTT has not, HTTP's tail is somewhere ahead
    'and has no business moving.
    If qn = QTailHttp Then
      QTailHttp = QTailHttp + 1
      If QTailHttp > Q_SIZE Then QTailHttp = 1
      qLost = True
    EndIf
    If qn = QTailMqtt Then
      QTailMqtt = QTailMqtt + 1
      If QTailMqtt > Q_SIZE Then QTailMqtt = 1
      'Only a loss if something was ever going to read that tail. With
      'MQTT_ENABLE false nothing advances it, so it sits on slot 1 and every
      'reading past the first Q_SIZE lands on it -- which used to make QDropped
      'climb on a station that had delivered every reading it ever took, and
      'QDropped is the one counter in the Diag table that is supposed to mean
      'something is wrong. The tail is still moved out of the way either way.
      If MQTT_ENABLE Then qLost = True
    EndIf
    If qLost Then QDropped = QDropped + 1
  EndIf

  qWhen(QHead) = NowWhen
  If pushChan = 0 Then
    qAddr(QHead) = RxLastId
    qVal(QHead) = RxLastValue
  Else
    qAddr(QHead) = -pushChan
    qVal(QHead) = pushRaw
  EndIf

  RdAddr = qAddr(QHead)
  RdValue = qVal(QHead)

  'The last step of the pipeline display, taken from the slot just written. The
  'radio half's six lines are MakeRec's, deliberately copied rather than called
  '-- see the note there. If you change one, change both.
  '
  'The local half has no JSON display of its own. v2.1 built one and it cost 200
  'bytes to say what LocalChan, LocalRaw and LocalEng already say between them,
  'while adding a second rendering that had to be kept identical to MakeRec's.
  If pushChan = 0 Then
    RxLastSuspect = (suspect = 1)
    Sprintf (dNum,"%d",RxLastId)
    RxLastJson = fId & dNum
    RxLastJson = RxLastJson & fTs & NowISO
    Sprintf (dNum,"%d",RxLastValue)
    RxLastJson = RxLastJson & fVal & dNum
    If RxLastSuspect Then RxLastJson = RxLastJson & fSus
    RxLastJson = RxLastJson & "}"
    RxStep = "7 queued"
    RxReadings = RxReadings + 1
  Else
    LocalChan = LocName(pushChan)
    LocalRaw = pushRaw
    If pushChan = LOC_RAIN Then
      LocalEng = pushRaw * Rain_bucket
    Else
      LocalEng = pushRaw
    EndIf
    LocalQueued = LocalQueued + 1
    SecsSinceLocal = 0
  EndIf

  CallTable Readings
  QHead = qn
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' LocalPush -- every staged local reading into the ring buffer.
'
' Called from the MAIN SCAN and from nowhere else, which is the whole reason
' this subroutine exists rather than the local sequences calling QueuePush
' themselves. QHead has exactly one writer by design -- the note at the ring
' buffer's declaration is built on it -- and a second sequence pushing readings
' would make that false in a way nothing would ever reproduce on a bench: two
' tasks incrementing QHead between each other's read and write, once a
' fortnight, costing one reading nobody can prove was ever taken.
'
' So the sequence that measures a channel only STAGES it: it writes locRawV and
' sets locPend, and the scan picks it up within a second. The handover is a flag
' rather than a queue of its own because the failure mode of a torn flag is
' bounded and harmless -- the reading is either sent twice (free, by contract)
' or sent a second later.
'
' Two things are refused rather than queued, and both would otherwise be stored
' forever:
'
'   NAN            -- an SDI-12 sensor that did not answer. FormatFloat renders
'                     it as the word "nan", which is not JSON, and one of those
'                     in a batch is a 400 for every reading in it.
'   past LOCAL_MAX -- a sensor that answered with nonsense. NAN is the honest
'                     failure; a mis-scaled or shorted sensor returns a NUMBER,
'                     and a number is what gets stored and later believed.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub LocalPush()
  For lk = 1 To LOC_N
    If locPend(lk) Then
      locPend(lk) = False

      If NOT ClockOK Then
        'Not counted as a rejection: nothing is wrong with the sensor, and the
        'clock state is already reported in one place. The reading is dropped
        'rather than held, because a stamp applied later would be a lie about
        'when it was measured.
        LocalState = "clock not set - nothing stamped"
      ElseIf locRawV(lk) = NAN Then
        LocalRejected = LocalRejected + 1
        LocalState = LocName(lk) & " read NAN - not queued"
      ElseIf ABS(locRawV(lk)) > LOCAL_MAX Then
        LocalRejected = LocalRejected + 1
        LocalState = LocName(lk) & " out of range - not queued"
      Else
        pushChan = lk
        pushRaw = locRawV(lk)
        Call QueuePush()
        LocalState = "queued " & LocName(lk)
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next lk
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TestInject -- one synthetic BINARY ALERT2 frame into the receive byte buffer.
'
' The live end-to-end test. Called from ReadSerial, one instruction before
' TakeFrames, so the bytes this writes are indistinguishable from the bytes
' SerialInBlock just wrote: the framer, DecodeFrame, the bit unpacking, the
' clock check, the queue, the batch builder and the POST all run on it exactly
' as they would on a real transmission. Nothing downstream is told this is a
' test, because a path that knows it is being tested is not the path under test.
'
' **Binary and not ASCII**, because this receiver speaks binary -- so DecodeFrame
' is the live decoder and ParseAlert2 is not, and a self-test that exercised the
' one nobody uses would be testing the wrong thing.
'
' Eighteen bytes, which is the shortest frame DecodeFrame will accept, and every
' one of them is there to satisfy a gate rather than to look plausible:
'
'   1-6    41 4C 45 52 54 32   "ALERT2", the signature TakeFrames scans for
'   7      0C                  the length byte: total = 6 + 12 = 18. Below
'                              FRAME_MIN_LEN it would be refused as not-a-length
'   8      00                  one pad byte. The anchor search starts at
'                              frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1, which is this byte
'   9-11   84 01 07            the anchor, and the element length
'   12     74                  ELEM_CONCENTRATION
'   13-14  <hi> <lo>           seconds since midnight, from this logger's clock,
'                              so RxFrameSkew comes out at 0
'   15-17  <b0> <b1> <b2>      the record: address and value, packed
'   18     00                  the status byte. Non-zero is a corrupt record
'
' The packing is DecodeFrame's table run backwards, and it is the one part worth
' checking by eye against that table:
'
'   b0 = address low 8 bits          address = (b1 MOD 32) * 256 + b0
'   b1 = address bits 12-8, plus     value   = INT(b1 / 32) * 256 + b2
'        value bits 10-8 times 32
'   b2 = value low 8 bits
'
' which is why TestId is bounded at 8191 and TestValue at 2047: 13 bits of
' address and 11 bits of value is all the wire carries, and a bigger number
' would silently wrap into the other field rather than fail.
'
' Injected only into an EMPTY byte buffer. Appending to a half-received real
' frame would put a second signature inside the first one's length, and the
' result would decode as one reading built out of two transmissions. So a busy
' station makes the shot wait for a gap and says so in TestState, rather than
' corrupting the thing it exists to prove works.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub TestInject()
  If TestEvery > 0 Then
    If TimeIntoInterval (0,TestEvery,Min) Then TestFire = True
  EndIf
  If NOT TestFire Then ExitSub

  If TestId < 1 OR TestId > 8191 Then
    TestState = "TestId must be 1-8191 - 13 bits on the wire"
    TestFire = False
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If TestValue < 0 OR TestValue > 2047 Then
    TestState = "TestValue must be 0-2047 - 11 bits on the wire"
    TestFire = False
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If nBuf > 0 Then
    'Stay armed. The buffer empties within a scan or two of the last frame.
    TestState = "waiting for a gap - the byte buffer is not empty"
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If NOT ClockOK Then
    'Refused rather than fired: the frame carries a time of day, and the reading
    'it produces would be refused at the queue's own clock gate anyway. "The
    'self-test did nothing" is a worse thing to read than the reason.
    TestState = "clock not set - nothing to stamp the frame with"
    TestFire = False
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  tstSecs = rTime(4) * 3600 + rTime(5) * 60 + rTime(6)
  tstB0 = TestId MOD 256
  tstB1 = INT(TestId / 256) + INT(TestValue / 256) * 32
  tstB2 = TestValue MOD 256

  tstFrame(1) = 65                               'A
  tstFrame(2) = 76                               'L
  tstFrame(3) = 69                               'E
  tstFrame(4) = 82                               'R
  tstFrame(5) = 84                               'T
  tstFrame(6) = 50                               '2
  tstFrame(7) = 12                               'length: 6 + 12 = 18 total
  tstFrame(8) = 0                                'pad
  tstFrame(9) = 132                              '84 -- the anchor
  tstFrame(10) = 1
  tstFrame(11) = 7                               'element length
  tstFrame(12) = ELEM_CONCENTRATION
  tstFrame(13) = INT(tstSecs / 256)
  tstFrame(14) = tstSecs MOD 256
  tstFrame(15) = tstB0
  tstFrame(16) = tstB1
  tstFrame(17) = tstB2
  tstFrame(18) = 0                               'status: a good record

  TestHex = ""
  For tstK = 1 To 18
    If nBuf < BUF_MAX Then
      nBuf = nBuf + 1
      rxByte(nBuf) = tstFrame(tstK)
    EndIf
    Sprintf (hxNum,"%02X ",tstFrame(tstK))
    TestHex = TestHex & hxNum
  Next tstK

  TestFire = False
  TestFired = TestFired + 1
  TestState = "fired - watch RxHex, RxLastId, RxStep, then QDepth"
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' RejectLine -- one place to record a frame or line that would not decode, so
' every rejection leaves the same evidence behind. Reason in RxLastWhy and its
' number in whyCode before the call.
'
'   1 short line              8 clock not set
'   2 frame flagged invalid   9 alert id out of range
'   3 payload len vs hex     10 unrecognised line
'   4 payload size           12 fields are not numbers
'   5 not whole records      13 no element in frame
'   6 payload not hex        14 element len not records
'   7 not concentration elem
'
' The per-reason tally v2.1 kept (RxWhyCount(14)) came out at v3.0: "which
' rejection, how many times" is the question a capture is run to answer, and the
' capture went with it. whyCode still says WHICH, on the last one, and Diag
' carries RxBadFrames every five minutes so the rate is still visible.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub RejectLine()
  RxLastBad = Mid(RxLine,1,120)
  RxBadFrames = RxBadFrames + 1
  RxStep = "rejected: " & RxLastWhy
EndSub

Sub ParsePlain()
  RxLastRecHex = ""

  'Both fields must be numbers before either is read as one. See IsNum.
  numIn = fld(1)
  Call IsNum()
  If numOK Then
    numIn = fld(2)
    Call IsNum()
  EndIf
  If NOT numOK Then
    RxLastWhy = "fields are not numbers - not this protocol"
    whyCode = 12
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxLastId = fld(1)                              'CRBasic converts the text on
  RxLastValue = fld(2)                           'assignment to a numeric

  If RxLastId < 1 OR RxLastId > 65535 Then
    RxLastWhy = "alert id outside 1-65535"
    whyCode = 9
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  Call StampNow()
  If NOT ClockOK Then
    RxLastWhy = "clock not set - not stamping"
    whyCode = 8
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxFrames = RxFrames + 1
  RxFrameSkew = NAN                              'no frame clock in this shape
  RxStep = "6 reading decoded"
  suspect = 0
  If RxLastValue = FULL_SCALE Then suspect = 1
  pushChan = 0                                   'a radio reading
  Call QueuePush()
EndSub

Sub ParseAlert2()
  RxLastRecHex = ""

  If nFld < N_FIELDS + 4 Then
    RxLastWhy = "short line - wrapped or truncated"
    whyCode = 1
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  'Field 18 was 1 on all 443 good frames in the reference capture and 0 on the
  'single corrupt one, whose records also carried addresses matching no station.
  'So this is a gate rather than a warning.
  frameOk = fld(18)
  If frameOk <> 1 Then
    RxLastWhy = "receiver flagged the frame invalid"
    whyCode = 2
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  'Field 23 is the payload length in bytes, and it matched the number of
  'trailing hex fields on all 444 frames -- so it is what tells a complete line
  'from one a narrow terminal wrapped.
  payLen = fld(23)
  nPay = nFld - N_FIELDS
  If payLen <> nPay Then
    RxLastWhy = "payload length disagrees with the hex"
    whyCode = 3
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If nPay < 7 OR nPay > 32 Then
    RxLastWhy = "payload too short or too long"
    whyCode = 4
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  If ((nPay - 3) MOD 4) <> 0 Then
    RxLastWhy = "payload is not whole records"
    whyCode = 5
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  'Hex to bytes. pay(1) is payload byte 0 -- CRBasic arrays start at 1.
  For i = 1 To nPay
    hexIn = fld(N_FIELDS + i)
    Call HexByte()
    If hexVal < 0 OR hexVal > 255 Then
      RxLastWhy = "payload byte is not hex"
      whyCode = 6
      Call RejectLine()
      ExitSub
    EndIf
    pay(i) = hexVal
  Next i
  RxStep = "5 payload decoded"

  If pay(1) <> ELEM_CONCENTRATION Then
    RxLastWhy = "not an ALERT concentration element"
    whyCode = 7
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  Call StampNow()
  If NOT ClockOK Then
    RxLastWhy = "clock not set - not stamping"
    whyCode = 8
    Call RejectLine()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxFrames = RxFrames + 1

  'The frame's own clock, against ours. See the note above.
  frameSecs = pay(2) * 256 + pay(3)
  loggerSecs = rTime(4) * 3600 + rTime(5) * 60 + rTime(6)
  RxFrameSkew = frameSecs - loggerSecs
  If RxFrameSkew > 43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew - 86400
  If RxFrameSkew < -43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew + 86400

  For i = 4 To nPay Step 4
    Sprintf (RxLastRecHex,"%02X %02X %02X %02X",pay(i),pay(i + 1),pay(i + 2),pay(i + 3))
    If pay(i + 3) <> 0 Then
      'A non-zero status byte marked corrupt records in the reference capture,
      'and those records carried addresses matching no station. Counted, not
      'posted.
      RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
    Else
      RxLastId = (pay(i + 1) MOD 32) * 256 + pay(i)
      RxLastValue = INT(pay(i + 1) / 32) * 256 + pay(i + 2)
      RxStep = "6 reading decoded"
      If RxLastId >= 1 AND RxLastId <= 65535 Then
        suspect = 0
        If RxLastValue = FULL_SCALE Then suspect = 1
        pushChan = 0                               'a radio reading
        Call QueuePush()
      Else
        RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next i
EndSub

Sub BufDrop()
  If dropN >= nBuf Then
    nBuf = 0
    ExitSub
  EndIf
  For dk2 = 1 To nBuf - dropN
    rxByte(dk2) = rxByte(dk2 + dropN)
  Next dk2
  nBuf = nBuf - dropN
EndSub

Sub TakeTextLine()
  tTook = False
  tEnd = 0
  tOK = True
  For tk2 = 1 To nBuf
    tb = rxByte(tk2)
    If tb = 13 OR tb = 10 Then
      tEnd = tk2
      If tb = 13 Then
        RxTermCR = RxTermCR + 1
      Else
        RxTermLF = RxTermLF + 1
      EndIf
      ExitFor
    EndIf
    If tb <> 9 AND (tb < 32 OR tb > 126) Then tOK = False
  Next tk2

  If tEnd = 0 Then
    'Nothing complete in there. Guard the buffer so a feed that never presents
    'anything framable cannot wedge it.
    If nBuf > BUF_MAX - RX_BLOCK Then
      RxBufDrops = RxBufDrops + 1
      dropN = nBuf
      Call BufDrop()
      tTook = True
    EndIf
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  tTook = True
  If NOT tOK Then
    RxNonText = RxNonText + 1
    dropN = tEnd
    Call BufDrop()
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxLine = ""
  For tk2 = 1 To tEnd - 1
    RxLine = RxLine & CHR(rxByte(tk2))
  Next tk2
  dropN = tEnd
  Call BufDrop()
  If Len(RxLine) > 0 Then Call ParseLine()
EndSub

Sub DecodeFrame()
  RxFrames = RxFrames + 1
  FrElemOff = 0
  FrElemVia = 0
  FrElemLen = 0
  FrTimeSecs = 0
  FrRecords = 0
  FrGood = 0
  FrBad = 0
  whyCode = 0
  RxLastWhy = ""

  hxFrom = frSigAt
  hxN = 40
  Call HexDump()
  RxStep = "5 frame captured"

  'The anchor: 84 01 <len> 74. Present on every frame in the reference capture,
  'at the same offset, and the length it carries satisfied (len-3) MOD 4 = 0 on
  'all of them.
  frElemAt = 0
  For fk = frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1 To frEnd - 4
    If rxByte(fk) = 132 Then
      If rxByte(fk + 1) = 1 AND rxByte(fk + 3) = ELEM_CONCENTRATION Then
        FrElemLen = rxByte(fk + 2)
        frElemAt = fk + 3
        FrElemVia = 1
        ExitFor
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next fk

  'Fallback: any 0x74 whose preceding byte is a plausible element length. Looser,
  'and reported as such, so a frame that does not carry the anchor still yields
  'its readings instead of yielding nothing.
  If frElemAt = 0 Then
    For fk = frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 2 To frEnd - 6
      If rxByte(fk) = ELEM_CONCENTRATION Then
        FrElemLen = rxByte(fk - 1)
        If FrElemLen >= 7 AND FrElemLen <= 32 Then
          If ((FrElemLen - 3) MOD 4) = 0 AND fk + FrElemLen - 1 <= frEnd Then
            frElemAt = fk
            FrElemVia = 2
            ExitFor
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    Next fk
  EndIf

  If frElemAt = 0 Then
    RxLastWhy = "no concentration element in frame"
    whyCode = 13
    RxBadFrames = RxBadFrames + 1
    RxStep = "rejected: " & RxLastWhy
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  FrElemOff = frElemAt - frSigAt + 1
  If FrElemLen < 7 OR FrElemLen > 32 OR ((FrElemLen - 3) MOD 4) <> 0 Then
    RxLastWhy = "element length is not whole records"
    whyCode = 14
    RxBadFrames = RxBadFrames + 1
    RxStep = "rejected: " & RxLastWhy
    ExitSub
  EndIf

  RxStep = "6 element decoded"

  'The frame's own clock, against ours -- the same comparison the ASCII path
  'makes, and the same open question. See ParseAlert2.
  FrTimeSecs = rxByte(frElemAt + 1) * 256 + rxByte(frElemAt + 2)
  loggerSecs = rTime(4) * 3600 + rTime(5) * 60 + rTime(6)
  RxFrameSkew = FrTimeSecs - loggerSecs
  If RxFrameSkew > 43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew - 86400
  If RxFrameSkew < -43200 Then RxFrameSkew = RxFrameSkew + 86400

  FrRecords = (FrElemLen - 3) / 4
  For frRec = frElemAt + 3 To frElemAt + FrElemLen - 1 Step 4
    Sprintf (RxLastRecHex,"%02X %02X %02X %02X",rxByte(frRec),rxByte(frRec + 1),rxByte(frRec + 2),rxByte(frRec + 3))

    'Same unpacking as the ASCII path: the middle byte carries the top three
    'bits of the value and the top five bits of the address. Read straight out
    'of the byte buffer -- v2.1 copied the four bytes into four Publics so a
    'table could sample them, and that table went at v3.0.
    RxLastId = (rxByte(frRec + 1) MOD 32) * 256 + rxByte(frRec)
    RxLastValue = INT(rxByte(frRec + 1) / 32) * 256 + rxByte(frRec + 2)

    If rxByte(frRec + 3) <> 0 Then
      FrBad = FrBad + 1
      RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
    ElseIf RxLastId < 1 OR RxLastId > 65535 Then
      FrBad = FrBad + 1
      RxBadRecords = RxBadRecords + 1
    ElseIf NOT ClockOK Then
      FrBad = FrBad + 1
      RxLastWhy = "clock not set - not stamping"
      whyCode = 8
    Else
      suspect = 0
      If RxLastValue = FULL_SCALE Then suspect = 1
      pushChan = 0                                 'a radio reading
      Call QueuePush()
      FrGood = FrGood + 1
    EndIf
  Next frRec

EndSub

Sub TakeFrames()
  frLoops = 0
  Do
    frLoops = frLoops + 1
    If frLoops > FRAME_LOOPS Then ExitDo
    frDone = False

    frSigAt = 0
    If nBuf >= SIG_LEN Then
      For fk = 1 To nBuf - SIG_LEN + 1
        If rxByte(fk) = 65 Then
          If rxByte(fk + 1) = 76 AND rxByte(fk + 2) = 69 AND rxByte(fk + 3) = 82 Then
            If rxByte(fk + 4) = 84 AND rxByte(fk + 5) = 50 Then
              frSigAt = fk
              ExitFor
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
      Next fk
    EndIf

    'Which form is it? "ALERT2A," is a letter A followed by a comma; a binary
    'frame's byte 7 is a length, and no observed frame has a length of 65 with a
    'comma behind it. Worked out before the chain below so the chain stays a
    'plain If/ElseIf -- there is no loop-continue statement in this program,
    'deliberately, because that is one more construct that cannot be checked
    'here before it reaches a compiler.
    frIsAscii = False
    If frSigAt > 0 Then
      If nBuf >= frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1 Then
        If rxByte(frSigAt + SIG_LEN) = 65 AND rxByte(frSigAt + SIG_LEN + 1) = 44 Then frIsAscii = True
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If frSigAt = 0 Then
      'Nothing framable. Try a text line instead, and stop when a pass consumes
      'nothing -- otherwise an empty run, such as the LF left over from a CRLF,
      'would be found again on every iteration.
      Call TakeTextLine()
      If NOT tTook Then frDone = True

    ElseIf nBuf < frSigAt + SIG_LEN Then
      frDone = True                                'the length byte is not here yet

    ElseIf frIsAscii Then
      RxFrameMode = "ascii"
      Call TakeTextLine()
      If NOT tTook Then frDone = True

    Else
      RxFrameMode = "binary"
      FrLenByte = rxByte(frSigAt + SIG_LEN)
      FrTotal = SIG_LEN + FrLenByte
      frEnd = frSigAt + FrTotal - 1

      If FrLenByte < FRAME_MIN_LEN OR FrTotal > FRAME_MAX Then
        'Not a length. Step past this signature byte and keep looking, rather
        'than trusting it and swallowing everything behind it.
        dropN = frSigAt
        Call BufDrop()
      ElseIf nBuf < frEnd Then
        frDone = True                              'wait for the rest of it
      Else
        Call DecodeFrame()
        dropN = frEnd
        Call BufDrop()
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If frDone Then ExitDo
  Loop
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ParseLine -- one complete TEXT line in RxLine, zero or more readings queued.
'
' Two shapes, told apart by what the line starts with, the same way alert2.js
' sniffs a capture. Sniffing on the raw text rather than on a split field means
' a line whose delimiters are wrong is still identified before it is blamed.
'
' **The delimiter is a comma**, and v3.0 says so instead of polling for it. v1
' carried a census of eight separator classes and a PickDelim that chose between
' five of them, because nobody knew what the feed was delimited by. It is
' ALERT2 ASCII, it is comma-delimited, and the census cost 1.4 KB to keep
' answering a question that has one answer. The fallback to a space is kept
' because it costs two lines and covers the one variant anybody has seen.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ParseLine()
  RxLastWhy = ""
  RxLastShape = ""
  whyCode = 0

  'Trim any trailing whitespace the terminator left behind, so the last field is
  'a clean number whichever of CR, LF or CRLF the receiver ends its lines with.
  Do While Len(RxLine) > 0
    tk = ASCII(Mid(RxLine,Len(RxLine),1))
    If tk = 13 OR tk = 10 OR tk = 32 OR tk = 9 Then
      RxLine = Mid(RxLine,1,Len(RxLine) - 1)
    Else
      ExitDo
    EndIf
  Loop
  If Len(RxLine) = 0 Then ExitSub

  RxLines = RxLines + 1
  RxLastLine = RxLine

  'Comma unless there is not one, in which case space. Anything else is a feed
  'this program does not claim to read, and it will fall out as "unrecognised"
  'below rather than being split on a guess.
  delim = ","
  aFound = 0
  For ak = 1 To Len(RxLine)
    If Mid(RxLine,ak,1) = "," Then
      aFound = 1
      ExitFor
    EndIf
  Next ak
  If aFound = 0 Then delim = " "
  Call SplitLine()
  RxLastFields = nFld
  RxStep = "4 line captured"

  'ALERT2A found anywhere in the line, not only at the front. A receiver that
  'prefixes its output -- a prompt, a sequence number, a stray byte from the
  'previous frame -- would otherwise be unrecognisable.
  aFound = 0
  If Len(RxLine) >= 7 Then
    For ak = 1 To Len(RxLine) - 6
      If Mid(RxLine,ak,7) = "ALERT2A" Then
        aFound = ak
        ExitFor
      EndIf
    Next ak
  EndIf
  If aFound > 0 Then
    If aFound > 1 Then
      RxLine = Mid(RxLine,aFound,Len(RxLine) - aFound + 1)
      Call SplitLine()
      RxLastFields = nFld
    EndIf
    RxLastShape = "ALERT2A"
    Call ParseAlert2()
  ElseIf nFld >= 2 Then
    RxLastShape = "plain"
    Call ParsePlain()
  Else
    RxLastShape = "unrecognised"
    RxLastWhy = "unrecognised line"
    whyCode = 10
    Call RejectLine()
  EndIf
EndSub

'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' ReadSerial -- the port, once per scan, into the byte buffer.
'
' SerialInBlock takes everything the port has and returns how many bytes that
' was, which is the count the loop below is bounded by. This is the KDO driver's
' read, and it replaces the SerialInRecord call that made the first version of
' this program silently deaf.
'
' SerialFlush is deliberately NOT called here. The KDO driver flushes because it
' owns a request/response conversation and anything left over is stale. This
' program is a listener on a feed it does not drive, and a flush would throw
' away the bytes that arrived while the scan was working.
'
' v3.0 removed the block hex dump, the block text dump, the buffer dumps and the
' capture-file write from this subroutine. What is left is the read and the
' append, which is all of it that ever touched a reading -- RxHex now dumps the
' bytes that became a FRAME, which is the copy worth having.
'------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub ReadSerial()
  RxAvail = SerialInChk (RX_PORT)
  RxBlockBytes = SerialInBlock (RX_PORT,RxBuf,RX_BLOCK)

  If RxBlockBytes > 0 Then
    RxBytesTotal = RxBytesTotal + RxBlockBytes
    SecsSinceRx = 0
    RxStep = "1 bytes read"

    'Into the byte buffer, raw. Every byte, including NUL, including CR. Read
    'through Mid() on the string SerialInBlock filled, bounded by the count it
    'returned rather than by Len() -- a NUL would end a CRBasic string early and
    'this feed carries nine to eleven of them per frame.
    For dk2 = 1 To RxBlockBytes
      If nBuf < BUF_MAX Then
        nBuf = nBuf + 1
        rxByte(nBuf) = ASCII(RxBuf(1,1,dk2))
        If rxByte(nBuf) = 0 Then RxNulls = RxNulls + 1
      Else
        RxBufDrops = RxBufDrops + 1
      EndIf
    Next dk2
    RxStep = "2 appended to byte buffer"
  Else
    SecsSinceRx = SecsSinceRx + 1
  EndIf

  'The self-test, if one is armed. Here and not in the scan above, so the bytes
  'it writes are appended by the same code path in the same instruction stream
  'as the bytes that just came off the port -- and are then framed by the same
  'call, on the same scan, with nothing able to tell the two apart.
  If TEST_ENABLE Then Call TestInject()

  Call TakeFrames()
  RxBufLen = nBuf
EndSub

Sub BuildBatch()
  'The envelope, rebuilt here rather than once in BeginProg, because BASE_NAME
  'is Public and a name changed at the logger has to reach the next POST.
  '
  'Quotes, backslashes and control characters are replaced on the way in. The
  'name lands inside a JSON string literal, and one of those typed into a Public
  'field would turn every batch into a 400 with nothing on the logger to say
  'why -- a failure the Const version could not have.
  jName = ""
  For nk2 = 1 To Len(BASE_NAME)
    nc2 = ASCII(Mid(BASE_NAME,nk2,1))
    If nc2 = 34 OR nc2 = 92 OR nc2 < 32 Then
      jName = jName & "_"
    Else
      jName = jName & CHR(nc2)
    EndIf
  Next nk2

  jEnvelope = DQ & "path" & DQ & ":" & DQ & jName & DQ & ","
  jEnvelope = jEnvelope & DQ & "protocol" & DQ & ":" & DQ & PROTOCOL & DQ & ","
  jEnvelope = jEnvelope & DQ & "readings" & DQ & ":["

  If bWrap Then
    ContentsString = "{" & DQ & "payload" & DQ & ":{" & jEnvelope
  Else
    ContentsString = "{" & jEnvelope
  EndIf

  BatchN = 0
  bi = bFrom

  Do While bi <> QHead AND BatchN < MAX_BATCH
    mi = bi
    Call MakeRec()
    If BatchN > 0 Then jRec = "," & jRec

    If Len(ContentsString) + Len(jRec) > CONTENT_GUARD Then ExitDo

    ContentsString = ContentsString & jRec
    BatchN = BatchN + 1
    bi = bi + 1
    If bi > Q_SIZE Then bi = 1
  Loop

  If bWrap Then
    ContentsString = ContentsString & jTail
  Else
    ContentsString = ContentsString & jTailBare
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub JsonInt()
  jsonVal = -1
  srcStr = HTTPResponse
  needle = DQ & jsonKey & DQ & ":"
  Call FindStr()
  If foundAt > 0 Then
    jp = foundAt + Len(needle)
    Do While jp <= Len(srcStr)
      jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
      If jd <> 32 AND jd <> 9 Then ExitDo
      jp = jp + 1
    Loop

    jd = 0
    If jp <= Len(srcStr) Then jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
    If jd >= 48 AND jd <= 57 Then
      jsonVal = 0
      Do While jp <= Len(srcStr)
        jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
        If jd < 48 OR jd > 57 Then ExitDo
        jsonVal = jsonVal * 10 + (jd - 48)
        jp = jp + 1
      Loop
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub ParseStatus()
  LastStatus = 0
  srcStr = RespHeader
  needle = "HTTP/"
  Call FindStr()
  If foundAt > 0 Then
    pp = foundAt
    Do While pp <= Len(srcStr)                   'step over "HTTP/1.1"
      If Mid(srcStr,pp,1) = " " Then ExitDo
      pp = pp + 1
    Loop
    pp = pp + 1
    Do While pp <= Len(srcStr)
      pd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,pp,1))
      If pd < 48 OR pd > 57 Then ExitDo
      LastStatus = LastStatus * 10 + (pd - 48)
      pp = pp + 1
    Loop
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub LoadToken()
  IngestToken = ""
  TokenOK = False
  TokenLen = 0
  fh = FileOpen (TOKEN_FILE,"r",0)
  If fh <> 0 Then
    FileReadLine (fh,IngestToken,80)
    FileClose (fh)

    'Trim whatever the editor that wrote the file left on the end. A trailing
    'newline here is not cosmetic: it would terminate the header block early and
    'the three headers after it would never be sent.
    Do While Len(IngestToken) > 0
      ltk = ASCII(Mid(IngestToken,Len(IngestToken),1))
      If ltk = 13 OR ltk = 10 OR ltk = 32 OR ltk = 9 Then
        IngestToken = Mid(IngestToken,1,Len(IngestToken) - 1)
      Else
        ExitDo
      EndIf
    Loop

    TokenLen = Len(IngestToken)
    If TokenLen > 0 Then TokenOK = True
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub PostBatch()
  PostAttempts = PostAttempts + 1

  HTTPHeader = "apikey: " & API_KEY & CRLF
  HTTPHeader = HTTPHeader & "X-Ingest-Token: " & IngestToken & CRLF
  HTTPHeader = HTTPHeader & "Content-Type: application/json" & CRLF
  HTTPHeader = HTTPHeader & "Content-Profile: meganet" & CRLF

  HTTPResponse = ""

  LastSocket = HTTPPost (INGEST_URI,ContentsString,HTTPResponse,HTTPHeader)

  RespHeader = HTTPHeader                        'the response headers, now
  Call ParseStatus()

  jsonKey = "accepted"
  Call JsonInt()

  If jsonVal >= 0 Then
    LastAccepted = jsonVal
    Accepted = Accepted + jsonVal

    jsonKey = "duplicates"
    Call JsonInt()
    If jsonVal < 0 Then jsonVal = 0
    LastDuplicates = jsonVal
    Duplicates = Duplicates + jsonVal

    'Every reading in a batch is accepted, a duplicate, or rejected -- the
    'contract leaves no fourth outcome -- so the rejected count is arithmetic
    'and the array does not need counting. What the array is read for is the
    'first reason, because the reasons repeat and docs/ingest-http.md tabulates
    'what each one usually means.
    LastRejected = BatchN - LastAccepted - LastDuplicates
    If LastRejected < 0 Then LastRejected = 0
    Rejected = Rejected + LastRejected

    LastReject = ""
    If LastRejected > 0 Then
      'Same whitespace problem as JsonInt: the rendered body is `"why": "..."`,
      'so the needle stops at the key and the colon, spaces and opening quote
      'are stepped over here.
      srcStr = HTTPResponse
      needle = DQ & "why" & DQ
      Call FindStr()
      If foundAt > 0 Then
        jp = foundAt + Len(needle)
        Do While jp <= Len(srcStr)
          jd = ASCII(Mid(srcStr,jp,1))
          If jd <> 58 AND jd <> 32 AND jd <> 9 AND jd <> 34 Then ExitDo
          jp = jp + 1
        Loop
        LastReject = Mid(HTTPResponse,jp,150)
      EndIf
    EndIf

    'Stored. Release exactly what was sent, from THIS path's tail -- QHead may
    'have moved on since, and the publisher's tail is none of this sequence's
    'business at this point in the scan.
    QTailHttp = QTailHttp + BatchN
    Do While QTailHttp > Q_SIZE
      QTailHttp = QTailHttp - Q_SIZE
    Loop

    PostOK = PostOK + 1
    ConsecFail = 0
    Backoff = 0
    SecsSincePost = 0
    If LastRejected > 0 Then
      PostState = "accepted, some rejected"
    Else
      PostState = "accepted"
    EndIf

  Else
    'No "accepted" in the body. Either the request never landed, or the endpoint
    'refused it -- 401 for a token it does not know, 400 for a body it could not
    'read. Both come back as a JSON error object, so the first characters of it
    'are worth more than any code this program could invent.
    PostFail = PostFail + 1
    ConsecFail = ConsecFail + 1
    LastReject = Mid(HTTPResponse,1,150)
    LastAccepted = 0
    LastDuplicates = 0
    LastRejected = 0

    If LastSocket = 0 Then
      PostState = "no answer - check link and TLS"
    ElseIf LastSocket = -2 Then
      PostState = "HTTPPost did not execute"
    ElseIf LastStatus = 401 Then
      PostState = "refused - token"
    ElseIf LastStatus = 400 Then
      PostState = "refused - malformed body"
    ElseIf LastStatus > 0 Then
      Sprintf (pNum,"%d",LastStatus)
      PostState = "refused - HTTP " & pNum
    Else
      PostState = "no usable answer - see RespHeader"
    EndIf

    If Backoff = 0 Then
      Backoff = BACKOFF_MIN
    Else
      Backoff = Backoff * 2
      If Backoff > BACKOFF_MAX Then Backoff = BACKOFF_MAX
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub MqttFailed()
  MqttFail = MqttFail + 1
  MqttConsecFail = MqttConsecFail + 1

  'The session is gone or was never made, so whatever the broker is holding as
  'this station's retained status is now the Last Will. Re-publishing it is how
  'the station says it is back, and there is no connect event in CRBasic to hang
  'that on.
  statusDue = True

  If MqttBackoff = 0 Then
    MqttBackoff = MQTT_BACKOFF_MIN
  Else
    MqttBackoff = MqttBackoff * 2
    If MqttBackoff > MQTT_BACKOFF_MAX Then MqttBackoff = MQTT_BACKOFF_MAX
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub MqttWorked()
  MqttConsecFail = 0
  MqttBackoff = 0
EndSub

Sub PublishBatch()
  MqttAttempts = MqttAttempts + 1
  MqttLastBatch = BatchN
  MqttLastPayload = Mid(ContentsString,1,200)
  SecsSinceTry = 0

  MqttResult = MQTTPublish (MqttTopic,ContentsString,MQTT_QOS,MQTT_DROP)

  If MqttResult = 0 Then
    'Handed over. Release exactly what was published, from this path's tail.
    QTailMqtt = QTailMqtt + MqttLastBatch
    Do While QTailMqtt > Q_SIZE
      QTailMqtt = QTailMqtt - Q_SIZE
    Loop

    MqttOK = MqttOK + 1
    MqttPublished = MqttPublished + MqttLastBatch
    SecsSinceMqtt = 0
    MqttState = "published"
    Call MqttWorked()
  Else
    Sprintf (mNum,"%d",MqttResult)
    MqttState = "broker refused or unreachable - code " & mNum
    Call MqttFailed()
  EndIf
EndSub

Sub PublishStatus()
  MqttStatusBody = "{" & DQ & "online" & DQ & ":true"
  If Batt_volt > 0 AND Batt_volt < 100 Then
    Sprintf (mNum,"%.2f",Batt_volt)
    MqttStatusBody = MqttStatusBody & "," & DQ & "battery_v" & DQ & ":" & mNum
  EndIf
  MqttStatusBody = MqttStatusBody & "," & DQ & "fw" & DQ & ":" & DQ & FW_VERSION & DQ
  MqttStatusBody = MqttStatusBody & "}"

  MqttAttempts = MqttAttempts + 1
  SecsSinceTry = 0

  MqttStatusResult = MQTTPublish (MqttStatusTopic,MqttStatusBody,MQTT_QOS,MQTT_KEEP)

  If MqttStatusResult = 0 Then
    SecsSinceStatus = 0
    statusDue = False
    Call MqttWorked()
  Else
    'Counted into the same backoff as a failed batch, and this is the case that
    'needs it most: a base station with an empty queue has no readings to knock
    'on the broker with, so this message IS the probe, and an un-backed-off
    'probe is a blocking publish every slow scan for as long as the broker is
    'down. MqttState is left saying whatever the readings last said, because a
    'failed status message adds nothing a failed batch has not already said.
    Call MqttFailed()
  EndIf
EndSub


'==============================================================================
' 6. MAIN PROGRAM
'==============================================================================

BeginProg

  DQ = CHR(34)
  CRLF = CHR(13) & CHR(10)

  'The per-reading JSON fragments, built once. The comment beside each
  'declaration above shows what it contains. jEnvelope is NOT built here -- it
  'carries BASE_NAME, which is Public, so BuildBatch rebuilds it per POST.
  '
  '`source` is deliberately absent from the envelope: ingest_http() stamps it
  '"http" itself, and a value repeated here could only ever disagree with the
  'truth.
  jTail = "]}}"
  jTailBare = "]}"
  fId   = "{" & DQ & "alert_id" & DQ & ":"
  fTs   = "," & DQ & "reading_ts" & DQ & ":" & DQ
  fVal  = DQ & "," & DQ & "value_raw" & DQ & ":"
  fSus  = "," & DQ & "quality" & DQ & ":" & DQ & "suspect" & DQ

  'And the local shape's fragments. fSn opens the station number's quote, fCh
  'closes it and opens the channel's -- MakeRec closes that one, because the
  'channel name comes out of a table and the fragment cannot hold it.
  fSn   = "{" & DQ & "station_number" & DQ & ":" & DQ
  fCh   = DQ & "," & DQ & "channel" & DQ & ":" & DQ
  fEng  = "," & DQ & "value" & DQ & ":"
  fUnit = "," & DQ & "unit" & DQ & ":" & DQ
  fConv = "," & DQ & "conversion" & DQ & ":" & DQ
  fProto = "," & DQ & "protocol" & DQ & ":" & DQ & LOCAL_PROTOCOL & DQ

  'The topics, built once from the two segments in section 1 rather than typed
  'out, so the scheme appears in this program exactly once. They are Public: at
  'commissioning, reading MqttTopic off the logger and checking it against the
  'line in the broker's ACL is the whole of the topic check.
  MqttTopic = "meganet/v1/" & MQTT_STATION & "/" & MQTT_DEVICE & "/reading"
  MqttStatusTopic = "meganet/v1/" & MQTT_STATION & "/status"

  'The local channel table. These names are the `channel` values on the wire and
  'therefore half of each reading's address in the database -- change one and the
  'readings before and after the change are two different addresses that no
  'query joins back together. They have to match the sensor rows in
  'db/migrations/0026_bateson_test_rig.sql, and `npm run logger` is what holds
  'the two files to each other.
  '
  'LocRawDp is the decimals value_raw is rendered with. It is 0 for rain because
  'that channel's raw is a tip COUNT, and three tips written as 3.000 is a raw
  'count pretending to have been measured to a thousandth.
  LocName(LOC_RAIN)    = "rain"
  LocUnit(LOC_RAIN)    = "mm"
  LocRawDp(LOC_RAIN)   = 0
  LocName(LOC_LEVEL1)  = "level_1"
  LocUnit(LOC_LEVEL1)  = "m"
  LocRawDp(LOC_LEVEL1) = 3
  LocName(LOC_LEVEL2)  = "level_2"
  LocUnit(LOC_LEVEL2)  = "m"
  LocRawDp(LOC_LEVEL2) = 3
  LocName(LOC_BATT)    = "battery"
  LocUnit(LOC_BATT)    = "V"
  LocRawDp(LOC_BATT)   = 3

  Level1_m = NAN                     'so a sensor that has never answered reads
  Level2_m = NAN                     'as NAN rather than as a plausible zero

  QHead = 1
  QTailHttp = 1
  QTailMqtt = 1
  QDepth = 0
  QDepthHttp = 0
  QDepthMqtt = 0
  Backoff = 0
  ConsecFail = 0
  MqttBackoff = 0
  MqttConsecFail = 0
  SecsSinceTry = MQTT_EVERY          'so the first status message goes out on the
                                     'first slow scan rather than 30 seconds in
  nBuf = 0
  RxFrameMode = "unknown"
  RxStep = "waiting for the first byte"
  PostState = "starting"

  If LOCAL_ENABLE Then
    LocalState = "starting"
  Else
    LocalState = "disabled - see LOCAL_ENABLE"
  EndIf
  If TEST_ENABLE Then
    TestState = "idle - set TestFire true to fire one"
  Else
    TestState = "disabled - see TEST_ENABLE"
  EndIf

  'Say hello as soon as there is a session to say it on. A restart is exactly
  'when the broker is still holding a will that says this station is down.
  statusDue = True
  If MQTT_ENABLE Then
    MqttState = "starting"
  Else
    MqttState = "disabled - see MQTT_ENABLE"
  EndIf

  Call LoadToken()

  'Once, here -- not in the scan. Re-opening the port every second re-allocates
  'the ring buffer and resets its pointers, discarding whatever arrived since
  'the last call, which on a busy base station is most of it.
  SerialOpen (RX_PORT,RX_BAUD,RX_FORMAT,RX_TXDELAY,RX_BUFFER)
  SerialFlush (RX_PORT)

  Call StampNow()

  '--- the fast half: listen --------------------------------------------------
  Scan (1,Sec,3,0)

    'PanelTemp's second parameter is the notch frequency -- the interference this
    'measurement rejects -- so it is the local mains frequency, 50 in Australia.
    PanelTemp (PTemp,50)
    Battery (Batt_volt)
    WatchdogErrs = Status.WatchdogErrors
    SkippedScans = Status.SkippedScan
    VarOutOfBound = Status.VarOutofBound

    Call StampNow()
    Call ReadSerial()

    '--- the local sensors, half of them ------------------------------------
    '
    ' The rain gauge is read HERE, in the one-second scan, and not in the
    ' sequence below with the SDI-12 sensors. PulseCount reports the tips that
    ' have arrived since the last time it was called IN THIS SCAN, so a call
    ' every five minutes would count correctly and a call in two places would
    ' not count at all -- and the instruction is a counter read, not a
    ' measurement, so it costs nothing to do it every second.
    '
    ' The SDI-12 sensors cannot be read here: SDI12Recorder blocks for about a
    ' second per sensor while the bus answers, and a base station that stops
    ' reading its serial port for two seconds has lost every transmission in
    ' that window. They are in a sequence of their own and STAGE their answers;
    ' LocalPush, below, is what actually queues them, in this scan, because
    ' QHead has one writer.
    If LOCAL_ENABLE Then
      PulseCount (Rain_tips,1,LOCAL_RAIN_CHAN,RAIN_CONFIG,RAIN_OPTION,1,0)
      Rain_tips_int = Rain_tips_int + Rain_tips
      Rain_mm = Rain_bucket * Rain_tips_int

      If TimeIntoInterval (0,LOCAL_EVERY,Min) Then
        'The rain gauge: this interval's tips. Only the RAW goes into the queue
        '-- MakeRec multiplies by the bucket when it renders, because the
        'conversion belongs to the channel rather than to the reading, and that
        'is what let the queue lose two of its seven columns.
        Rain_last_mm = Rain_mm
        Rain_total_mm = Rain_total_mm + Rain_mm
        locRawV(LOC_RAIN) = Rain_tips_int
        locPend(LOC_RAIN) = True
        Rain_tips_int = 0

        'The supply voltage, from the Battery instruction at the top of this
        'scan. It is here because it is the channel that needs nothing wired to
        'it: a rig with no sensors attached at all still reports something, so
        '"is this station reaching MegaNet" is answerable before anybody has
        'been to site.
        locRawV(LOC_BATT) = Batt_volt
        locPend(LOC_BATT) = True
      EndIf

      Call LocalPush()
    EndIf

    'How far behind each path is, and how much the buffer is therefore still
    'holding. QDepth is the greater of the two because a slot is only reusable
    'once BOTH have passed it -- so it, and not either half, is what QPeak and
    'Q_SIZE have to be read against.
    QDepthHttp = QHead - QTailHttp
    If QDepthHttp < 0 Then QDepthHttp = QDepthHttp + Q_SIZE
    QDepthMqtt = QHead - QTailMqtt
    If QDepthMqtt < 0 Then QDepthMqtt = QDepthMqtt + Q_SIZE

    If MQTT_ENABLE Then
      qLag = QDepthMqtt
    Else
      'Nothing is draining the publisher's tail, so counting it would report a
      'buffer that only fills. QueuePush still moves that tail out of the way.
      qLag = 0
    EndIf
    QDepth = QDepthHttp
    If qLag > QDepth Then QDepth = qLag
    If QDepth > QPeak Then QPeak = QDepth

    SecsSincePost = SecsSincePost + 1
    SecsSinceLocal = SecsSinceLocal + 1
    SecsSinceMqtt = SecsSinceMqtt + 1
    SecsSinceTry = SecsSinceTry + 1
    SecsSinceStatus = SecsSinceStatus + 1

    CallTable Diag

  NextScan

  '--- the slow half: talk, both ways -----------------------------------------
  '
  ' A sequence of its own because BOTH of the instructions in it block: HTTPPost
  ' sits there until the server answers or its 75-second timeout expires, and
  ' MQTTPublish at QoS 1 waits for the broker's PUBACK.
  '
  ' ONE sequence for both paths rather than one each, and that is the load-
  ' bearing decision in this file. Two sequences would let a slow POST and a slow
  ' publish overlap, and the two halves share BuildBatch, ContentsString, BatchN
  ' and MakeRec's scratch -- so overlapping them means a message occasionally
  ' built out of half of the other path's batch: a well-formed batch of the
  ' wrong readings, which is invisible.
  '
  ' What it costs is that a stalled POST delays a publish by up to 75 seconds.
  ' What it does not cost is progress: the two tails are independent, so the
  ' delayed path resumes from where it was and catches up in batches of
  ' MAX_BATCH.
  SlowSequence
    Scan (10,Sec,3,0)

      '--- path 1: POST ---------------------------------------------------
      postDue = False
      If QDepthHttp > 0 Then
        If ConsecFail = 0 Then
          If SecsSincePost >= POST_EVERY Then postDue = True
        Else
          If SecsSincePost >= Backoff Then postDue = True
        EndIf
      EndIf

      If NOT TokenOK Then
        PostState = "no token - see TOKEN_FILE"
        Call LoadToken()                 'so dropping the file in is enough,
        postDue = False                  'with no restart
      ElseIf NOT ClockOK Then
        PostState = "clock not set - nothing stamped"
        postDue = False
      ElseIf QDepthHttp = 0 Then
        PostState = "idle - nothing to send"
      EndIf

      If postDue Then
        bWrap = True                     'ingest_http() wants the payload wrapper
        bFrom = QTailHttp
        Call BuildBatch()
        If BatchN > 0 Then
          PostState = "posting"
          Call PostBatch()
        EndIf
      EndIf

      '--- path 2: PUBLISH ------------------------------------------------
      '
      ' No token test here, and that is not an omission: the bridge holds the
      ' ingest token for this path, and what this station needs instead is a
      ' broker credential, which lives in the MQTT settings where CRBasic cannot
      ' see it.
      If MQTT_ENABLE Then
        'The status message is due on its own timer, and after any failure --
        'armed here whether or not the broker is going to be touched this tick.
        If SecsSinceStatus >= MQTT_STATUS_EVERY Then statusDue = True

        'ONE gate over both messages: how long the broker is left alone. Both
        'are blocking publishes to the same connection, so letting either past
        'the backoff on its own would be a knock every ten seconds at exactly
        'the moment the link can least carry one. Measured from the last attempt
        'rather than the last success, because a backoff measured from a success
        'that has not happened for an hour has already expired every time it is
        'read.
        mqttWait = MQTT_EVERY
        If MqttConsecFail > 0 Then mqttWait = MqttBackoff

        If SecsSinceTry >= mqttWait Then
          mqttSent = False

          If NOT ClockOK Then
            'Belt and braces: QueuePush will not have queued anything unstamped.
            MqttState = "clock not set - nothing stamped"
          ElseIf QDepthMqtt > 0 Then
            bWrap = False                'the bridge builds the wrapper itself
            bFrom = QTailMqtt
            Call BuildBatch()
            If BatchN > 0 Then
              MqttState = "publishing"
              Call PublishBatch()
              mqttSent = True
            EndIf
          Else
            MqttState = "idle - nothing to send"
          EndIf

          'Readings first, then the status -- but only when the tick has not
          'already spent a blocking publish on a broker that refused it.
          If statusDue Then
            If NOT mqttSent OR MqttConsecFail = 0 Then Call PublishStatus()
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf

    NextScan
  EndSequence

  '--- the local sensors: the SDI-12 bus --------------------------------------
  '
  ' Its own sequence because SDI12Recorder blocks: it puts the address and the
  ' command on the bus and waits for the sensor to say it is ready and then to
  ' answer, which is about a second per sensor on a standard "M!". Two of them
  ' in the one-second main scan is two seconds of not reading the serial port,
  ' every interval, and every transmission in that window is gone.
  '
  ' It does NOT queue anything. It writes the values into the staging array and
  ' sets locPend; the main scan sees the flag within a second and calls
  ' QueuePush. QHead has one writer, and this is what keeps that true.
  '
  ' A sensor that does not answer leaves NAN in its variable, which is the
  ' honest answer and is what LocalPush refuses to queue. FillNaN is -1 here for
  ' exactly that: the alternative is the previous reading left in place, and a
  ' level sensor that has been unplugged for a week reporting last Tuesday's
  ' level is the one failure this whole program is written to avoid.
  SlowSequence
    Scan (LOCAL_EVERY,Min,1,0)
      If LOCAL_ENABLE Then
        SDI12Recorder (Level1_m,LOCAL_SDI_PORT,LVL1_SDI_ADDR,"M!",1.0,0,-1,1)
        SDI12Recorder (Level2_m,LOCAL_SDI_PORT,LVL2_SDI_ADDR,"M!",1.0,0,-1,1)
        locRawV(LOC_LEVEL1) = Level1_m
        locPend(LOC_LEVEL1) = True
        locRawV(LOC_LEVEL2) = Level2_m
        locPend(LOC_LEVEL2) = True
      EndIf
    NextScan
  EndSequence

  '--- clock discipline -------------------------------------------------------
  '
  ' Its own sequence because NetworkTimeProtocol blocks for up to 70 seconds,
  ' which would stall any of the three above. Five minutes between scans is
  ' comfortably longer than that, so this never overruns.
  '
  ' The return value is the clock error in milliseconds BEFORE the correction,
  ' and NAN when the sync failed. A NAN fails both halves of the range test
  ' below, which is why the test is written as a range rather than as a
  ' comparison against zero -- a good sync legitimately returns near zero, so
  ' testing for zero would conflate success with failure.
  SlowSequence
    Scan (300,Sec,1,0)
      SecsSinceNTP = SecsSinceNTP + 300
      If Len(NTP_SERVER) > 0 Then
        'NOT ClockVerified is what makes the first pass sync immediately and
        'keeps retrying every five minutes until it works.
        If TimeIntoInterval(0,NTP_EVERY_HR,Hr) OR NOT ClockVerified OR NOT ClockOK Then
          NTPErrMs = NetworkTimeProtocol (NTP_SERVER,0,1000)
          If NTPErrMs > -86400000 AND NTPErrMs < 86400000 Then
            NTPLastOK = True
            ClockVerified = True
            SecsSinceNTP = 0
          Else
            NTPLastOK = False
            ClockState = "NTP sync failed - see NTP_SERVER"
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    NextScan
  EndSequence

EndProg


'==============================================================================
' Other dataloggers
'==============================================================================
'
' CR1000X -- save as .CR1X. Check PanelTemp's second parameter against that
'   logger's help: on the CR300 it is the notch frequency in Hz, and on other
'   loggers the same position is an integration time or a _50Hz/_60Hz constant.
'   Check the Status fields too -- the CR1000X has more of them, including the
'   MemoryFree and SkippedSlowScan this program left out for the CR300's sake.
' CR6 -- save as .CR6. The RS-232 port is ComRS232; a serial daughterboard port
'   is ComC1 or ComC3 depending on how it is wired.
'
' The local sensors need two constants checked on either. LOCAL_RAIN_CHAN is
'   P_SW, which is the CR300's dedicated switched-pulse terminal and does not
'   exist on the other two -- there the tipping bucket goes on a C terminal (C1,
'   C2, ...) configured for pulse input, so the constant becomes that terminal's
'   name. LOCAL_SDI_PORT is C1 here; on a CR6 an SDI-12 bus can be U1/U2 as well.
'
' **Both have more memory than a CR300, and this file is the shape it is because
' a CR300 does not.** v2.1 would not fit on one. If you are moving to a CR1000X
' or a CR6, the things worth spending the extra memory on, in order: Q_SIZE (one
' slot is 12 bytes now, so 2,000 slots is 24 KB and about three days of outage),
' then CONTENT_SIZE/CONTENT_GUARD/MAX_BATCH together -- a local reading is up to
' 180 characters, and the ceiling is 1000 readings per message on BOTH paths.
' Bringing back the forensics tables and the raw capture (`git show ea4116d`) is
' the last thing to spend it on, not the first: they answer a question about the
' feed that this network has already answered.
'
' MQTTPublish() is the one instruction here that is not on every operating
' system. It IS on a CR300: the product manual (archive/cr300.pdf, section 13.3,
' printed page 149) lists it among the internet-communications instructions. A
' logger too old for it will not compile this file at all, and MQTT_ENABLE
' cannot help -- the compiler reads the whole program. On such a logger delete
' MqttFailed, MqttWorked, PublishBatch, PublishStatus and the "path 2" block in
' the slow sequence, and the HTTP half is exactly the program it was.
'
'==============================================================================
' What this program deliberately does not do
'==============================================================================
'
' It does not convert counts to millimetres or metres FOR THE RADIO. See point 4
'   at the top, and the paragraph under it about why the sensors on this
'   logger's own terminals are the one place that does.
'
' It does not make the self-test look like a test. The frame TestInject writes
'   is a valid binary ALERT2 frame and nothing downstream is told where it came
'   from, because a path that knows it is being tested is not the path under
'   test. What separates a self-test reading from a real one is its ADDRESS --
'   8101, in a block no station in the registry uses -- so it is
'   distinguishable in the database, by a query, after the fact, rather than
'   being handled differently on the way through.
'
' It does not fire the self-test on its own by default. TestEvery ships at 0.
'   An unattended self-test writes synthetic readings into the live database
'   forever, and a test rig that quietly becomes a data source is a worse
'   problem than the one it was solving.
'
' It does not keep a raw capture, a byte census or a per-line table any more.
'   See the version note at the top: those answered what the feed IS, and it is
'   answered. `git show ea4116d:logger/base-station-http.CR300` is the copy that
'   still has them, and it is the right starting point for a receiver nobody has
'   seen before -- on a logger with the memory to run it.
'
' It does not flush the serial port after reading it. A flush belongs to a
'   driver that owns a request/response conversation. Here the receiver talks
'   whenever it likes and a flush would discard the bytes that arrived while
'   the scan was working.
'
' It does not decode HFEM. An HFEM line (":HS=1|I1=..|NN:") is a different
'   format from a different kind of station, and hfem.js is the decoder for it.
'
' It does not stream a DataTable through HTTPPost's optional parameters. Those
'   produce TOB1, TOA5, CSIXML or CSIJSON, and ingest_http() accepts none of
'   them -- it wants one JSON object with a `payload` key.
'
' It does not use MQTTPublishTable, or the logger's own automatic publishing.
'   Those emit CSIJSON or GeoJSON on the MQTTBaseTopic, and the bridge subscribes
'   to a topic scheme and a payload contract that is neither.
'
' It does not set the Last Will. There is no CRBasic instruction for it -- the
'   will is sent in the CONNECT packet, which the operating system builds from
'   the MQTT settings. All this program can do is publish the other half of the
'   pair, and say loudly here and in logger/README.md that a base station whose
'   will is unset or left un-retained has no offline detection at all.
'
' It does not subscribe to anything, and it does not call MQTTConnect. The
'   operating system opens and holds the session on its own once MQTT is enabled
'   in the settings; the instruction exists to FORCE an attempt, which is for
'   programs that power a modem down between transmissions.
'
' It does not enforce which addresses this base may post for. Neither does the
'   endpoint: alert_low/alert_high on the token record are a note, not a rule
'   (db/migrations/0007_ingest_http.sql, decision 3).
